/* Birdbird – King Air B200 question bank. Own wording; facts checked against training material. Always verify against the AFM/POH and your operator's documents. */
window.B200Q=[
 {id:'gen-001',ch:'gen',lvl:1,q:'Which engines power the King Air B200, and what is each one\'s shaft-horsepower rating?',a:['Pratt & Whitney Canada PT6A-42, 850 shp each','Pratt & Whitney Canada PT6A-60A, 1,050 shp each','Pratt & Whitney Canada PT6A-42, 1,050 shp each','Garrett TPE331, 850 shp each'],c:0,e:'The B200 uses the PT6A-42 (the earlier 200 used the -41). Both versions are rated at 850 shp.'},
 {id:'gen-002',ch:'gen',lvl:1,q:'In what certification category is the B200, and which manoeuvres does that rule out?',a:['Normal category: spins are allowed only with the flaps up','Commuter category: no spins, but steep turns up to 90° are allowed','Utility category: limited aerobatics, but no spins','Normal category: no aerobatic manoeuvres, spins included'],c:3,e:'The B200 is a Normal-category aeroplane. No aerobatic manoeuvres are approved, and that includes intentional spins.'},
 {id:'gen-003',ch:'gen',lvl:1,q:'What is the approximate wingspan of the King Air B200?',a:['54 ft 6 in','43 ft 9 in','49 ft 8 in','57 ft 11 in'],c:0,e:'The span is 54 ft 6 in. About 43 ft 9 in is the overall length, so do not mix the two up.'},
 {id:'gen-004',ch:'gen',lvl:1,q:'What is the reference wing area of the B200?',a:['254 sq ft','280 sq ft','303 sq ft','350 sq ft'],c:2,e:'The wing area is 303 square feet. You need it, for example, when working out wing loading.'},
 {id:'gen-005',ch:'gen',lvl:2,q:'How and when is the inflatable seal around the cabin airstair door pressurised?',a:['With regulated bleed air through a 4 psi regulator, once the weight is off the wheels','With vacuum from the bleed-air ejector, as soon as both engines are running at idle','With cabin pressurisation air only, as soon as the door handle is locked','With hydraulic pressure from the gear power pack, once the gear retracts'],c:0,e:'Regulated pneumatic air passes a further pressure regulator of about 4 psi. A valve controlled by the left gear safety switch lets that air inflate the seal once the aeroplane is airborne.'},
 {id:'gen-006',ch:'gen',lvl:2,q:'Why does it take more force to press the door-handle release button while the cabin is pressurised?',a:['A solenoid locks the button whenever the battery switch is ON','A diaphragm in the button pushes back harder as cabin differential rises','The button is mechanically linked to the safety valve, which is held closed in flight','The latch bolts expand with cold soak at altitude and bind in their guides'],c:1,e:'The release button contains a pressure-sensing diaphragm. The higher the cabin differential, the harder the button is to press, which guards against opening the door by mistake in flight.'},
 {id:'gen-007',ch:'gen',lvl:3,q:'In cruise, the cabin door warning illuminates (red on the B200) and a passenger reports a faint whistle near the door. What is the correct approach?',a:['Continue normally and only act if the cabin altitude starts to climb or the warning begins to flash','Keep everyone seated and belted, descend as required, depressurise, and inspect only after landing (QRH)','Raise the cabin differential so the door is pressed more firmly onto its seal, then continue','Send a crew member back to check the latch handle and re-lock the door while still pressurised'],c:1,e:'Do not inspect or try to open the door while airborne. Keep the occupants seated and belted, descend as needed and depressurise, then inspect the door once the aeroplane is stopped. The QRH governs the detailed steps.'},
 {id:'gen-008',ch:'gen',lvl:2,q:'Which statement about the cabin emergency exit is correct?',a:['A plug-type hatch, forward right side; it comes into the cabin and must be unlocked for take-off','A door on the left side aft of the airstair, hinged at the top and held open by a pair of gas springs','A hatch in the cockpit roof that opens outwards on hinges and is used for ditching only','A plug-type hatch on the left side that can only be opened from outside once it is locked'],c:0,e:'The exit is a plug-type window hatch, forward on the right side. It can be opened from inside even when locked, but it must be unlocked before take-off so that rescuers can open it from outside.'},
 {id:'gen-009',ch:'gen',lvl:1,q:'How can you confirm that the cabin door latches are properly engaged?',a:['By pushing hard on the closed door from inside and listening for movement','Through the inspection ports: each latch bolt\'s stripe must line up with its pointer','By checking for a green DOOR LOCKED advisory on the caution-advisory panel','By watching that the cabin differential gauge rises slightly during the taxi out'],c:1,e:'A placard shows how to look through the inspection windows. On each latch bolt the painted stripe should line up with its black pointer.'},
 {id:'gen-010',ch:'gen',lvl:2,q:'Why should the control column pin, the rudder pin and the power-lever clamp always go on and come off together?',a:['Because the lever clamp also unlocks the nose-wheel steering once it is removed','Because the pins are wired into the starter circuit and block engine start until removed','So nobody can start, taxi or fly with free engine levers while the controls are locked','Because the rudder pin cannot be pulled out while the column pin is still fitted in place'],c:2,e:'The lock set is designed to be fitted and removed as a unit. The U-clamp over the engine levers is the visible reminder that the flight controls are locked, so you cannot start or taxi with controls locked by mistake.'},
 {id:'gen-011',ch:'gen',lvl:2,q:'Why must the oxygen PULL ON / SYS READY handle be pulled out before engine start?',a:['It only opens the first-aid outlet; crew masks are supplied regardless','It powers the PASS OXY ON annunciator test and the passenger mask-door test circuit','It arms the ALT WARN pressure switch, which otherwise stays inoperative in flight','Pushed in, no oxygen reaches anyone, and its cable could freeze in that position'],c:3,e:'The handle opens the bottle shut-off valve. Pushed in means no oxygen for crew, passengers or the first-aid outlet, and a frozen cable could make it impossible to pull later. So it is armed before flight.'},
 {id:'gen-012',ch:'gen',lvl:2,q:'With the oxygen system armed, when do the passenger masks deploy automatically?',a:['At about 12,500 ft cabin altitude, when the passenger valve opens automatically','Once the ALT WARN light has been on continuously for about 60 seconds or more','At 10,000 ft cabin altitude, after a 30-minute delay set by a timer in the controller','Only when the pilot pulls the passenger manual override handle'],c:0,e:'Above roughly 12,500 ft cabin altitude, a barometric switch opens the passenger supply valve. Line pressure pops the mask doors open, and the green PASS OXY ON advisory confirms oxygen is flowing to the passenger line.'},
 {id:'gen-013',ch:'gen',lvl:3,q:'You need oxygen duration for 2 crew on diluter-demand masks set to 100 % and 4 passengers on constant-flow masks. What \'number of people\' do you enter in the duration table?',a:['4','6','10','8'],c:3,e:'For the duration calculation, each diluter-demand mask used at 100 % counts as two people. So 2 crew × 2 + 4 passengers = 8.'},
 {id:'gen-014',ch:'gen',lvl:1,q:'Above what cabin altitude must the crew use the 100 % setting on their diluter-demand masks?',a:['20,000 ft','About 12,500 ft','About 10,000 ft','25,000 ft'],c:0,e:'Above a cabin altitude of 20,000 ft, the diluter (air-mix) mode cannot provide enough oxygen, so 100 % is required. The 100 % or emergency setting is also used when there is smoke or fumes.'},
 {id:'elec-001',ch:'elec',lvl:1,q:'What are the basic sources of DC power on a typical late-serial B200?',a:['A 24 V NiCad battery, two 150 A generators and a ram-air turbine','A 24 V 42 Ah lead-acid battery, two 250 A starter-generators, and a GPU socket','A 28 V 36 Ah NiCad battery, two 400 A alternators, and an external power socket','A 24 V lead-acid battery, one 300 A starter-generator and an APU in the tail cone'],c:1,e:'Later serials have a 24 V, 42 Ah lead-acid gel battery (earlier aircraft had a 34/36 Ah NiCad). Two 250 A starter-generators are regulated to 28.25 ±0.25 V, and a ground power unit can be connected.'},
 {id:'elec-002',ch:'elec',lvl:1,q:'What AC power do the inverters supply, and how many run at the same time?',a:['115 V at 60 Hz; both inverters run in parallel','26 V at 400 Hz only; both run continuously together','115 V and 26 V at 400 Hz; only one runs at a time','115 V and 26 V at 400 Hz; both run together, one per bus'],c:2,e:'Each inverter can supply both 115 V and 26 V at 400 Hz. The INVERTER selector powers one inverter at a time; the other is the standby.'},
 {id:'elec-003',ch:'elec',lvl:1,q:'What are the required limits for inverter output?',a:['105–120 V AC and 380–420 Hz','24–32 V AC and 400 Hz exactly','About 100–130 V AC and 390–410 Hz','110–115 V AC and 350–450 Hz'],c:0,e:'The avionics need 105–120 V AC at 380–420 Hz. You can check this on the AC volt-frequency meter, if one is fitted.'},
 {id:'elec-004',ch:'elec',lvl:1,q:'What is the starter duty cycle limit?',a:['60 s on, 60 s off; 60 s on, 60 s off; 60 s on, then 15 min off','40 s on, 5 min off; 40 s on, 5 min off; 40 s on, then 1 h off','30 s on, 2 min off; 30 s on, 2 min off; repeated without a limit','40 s on, 60 s off; 40 s on, 60 s off; 40 s on, then 30 min off'],c:3,e:'The starter-generator heats up quickly when cranking. Three 40-second cycles are allowed, with 60-second rests between the first two and a 30-minute cooling period after the third.'},
 {id:'elec-005',ch:'elec',lvl:1,q:'What is the maximum sustained generator load, in percent of rated output?',a:['100 % up to 31,000 ft, 88 % above 31,000 ft, 85 % on the ground','100 % at all altitudes in flight, 88 % on the ground with no N1 limits','90 % up to 31,000 ft, 75 % above 31,000 ft, and 100 % on the ground','85 % up to 25,000 ft, 100 % above 25,000 ft, 88 % on the ground'],c:0,e:'Generator cooling is less effective at very high altitude and on the ground. Hence the limit is 88 % above 31,000 ft and 85 % on the ground, with extra minimum-N1 rules for ground running.'},
 {id:'elec-006',ch:'elec',lvl:2,q:'The left loadmeter reads 0.5. How much current is that generator delivering?',a:['About 50 A, because it reads in tens of amperes','About 125 A, because it shows a fraction of 250 A','About 14 V, because it shows half of bus voltage','About 150 A, because it shows a fraction of 300 A'],c:1,e:'The loadmeters show load as a percentage of the generator\'s 250 A continuous rating, so 50 % is 125 A. Press the meter button to read bus voltage instead.'},
 {id:'elec-007',ch:'elec',lvl:2,q:'Why can the engine fire extinguishers and firewall shut-off valves be used even with the battery switch OFF?',a:['They have their own small emergency batteries in the nacelles','They are powered by the standby inverter whenever it is selected','They are pneumatically operated and need no electrical power','They are on the hot battery bus, fed directly by the battery'],c:3,e:'The hot battery bus is always connected to the battery, whatever the BAT switch position. That is why the extinguishers and firewall valves stay usable, and also why the battery can be drained if something on that bus is left on.'},
 {id:'elec-008',ch:'elec',lvl:2,q:'What links the left and right generator buses during normal operation?',a:['The isolation bus, through a 325 A current limiter on each side','A 50 A feeder breaker on the copilot\'s circuit-breaker panel','A single bus-tie relay that the pilot closes with a cockpit switch','Nothing; each side is always isolated unless crossfeed is selected'],c:0,e:'The two generator buses are tied together through the isolation bus by two 325 A current limiters. As long as both limiters are intact, the battery, both generators and all the buses act as one paralleled system.'},
 {id:'elec-009',ch:'elec',lvl:2,q:'Each dual-fed bus is fed from both generator buses through a current limiter, a diode and a circuit breaker. What is the diode for?',a:['To boost the dual-fed bus voltage automatically when one generator fails','To stop a faulty or low generator bus pulling the dual-fed bus down','To convert the inverter\'s AC output to DC for the dual-fed bus','To let the pilot choose which generator bus feeds the dual-fed bus with a switch'],c:1,e:'From each side the feed passes a 60 A limiter, a 70 A diode and a 50 A breaker. The diodes let current flow only towards the dual-fed bus, so a fault on one generator bus cannot pull the dual-fed bus (or the other side) down with it.'},
 {id:'elec-010',ch:'elec',lvl:2,q:'How are paired items, such as the left and right landing lights, spread across the dual-fed buses?',a:['Both on bus No. 1, so a single breaker can isolate both items together if one fails','Both on the hot battery bus, so they keep working with the battery switch off','Left on bus 1 or 3, right on bus 2 or 4, so one bus failure never takes both','Left on the right generator bus and right on the left, crossed over'],c:2,e:'Paired loads are deliberately split, left items on dual-fed bus 1 or 3 and right items on 2 or 4. A single bus failure then removes only one of the pair.'},
 {id:'elec-011',ch:'elec',lvl:2,q:'How do you bring a generator on line in an aircraft with the GEN RESET position?',a:['Cycle the battery switch OFF and ON, then select ON','Hold RESET until the loadmeter reads about 100 %','Hold RESET for at least one second, then release to ON','Select ON directly; holding RESET would trip the GCU'],c:2,e:'Holding RESET for at least a second resets the generator control unit. When the switch is released to ON, the GCU closes the line contactor if the generator output is correct.'},
 {id:'elec-012',ch:'elec',lvl:2,q:'What happens when you push down the hinged MASTER SWITCH bar (gang bar)?',a:['Only the avionics master and both inverters switch off together','Both generators reset, but the battery stays connected to the main bus','The inverters change over and the avionics buses reset','The battery and both generator switches go to OFF together'],c:3,e:'The gang bar sits over the BAT and GEN switches. Pushing it down turns off the battery and both generators in one movement.'},
 {id:'elec-013',ch:'elec',lvl:2,q:'Which conditions make a generator control unit take its generator off line?',a:['Low engine oil pressure or a chip-detector signal','Selecting the other inverter or switching the avionics master','Over- or under-voltage, or over- or under-excitation','Any loadmeter reading above 50 % for more than one minute'],c:2,e:'The GCU regulates voltage and protects the system. It trips the line contactor on over- or under-voltage and over- or under-excitation, and the amber L or R DC GEN caution comes on.'},
 {id:'elec-014',ch:'elec',lvl:1,q:'On a typical late-serial B200, which conditions must ALL be met before the external power relay closes?',a:['Correct polarity, battery switch ON, GPU no more than about 32 V','GPU plugged in, both generator switches ON, avionics master OFF','Battery switch OFF, so the battery is protected from the GPU','Correct polarity, avionics master ON and an inverter selected'],c:0,e:'The relay closes only with correct polarity, the BAT switch ON and the GPU voltage not above 32 V. Very early aircraft did not need the battery switch ON and had no over-voltage protection.'},
 {id:'elec-015',ch:'elec',lvl:2,q:'Why should ground power never be connected unless the aircraft battery shows at least about 20 V and is switched on?',a:['The GPU relay needs 20 V from the battery to open the firewall fuel valves','Avionics relays need voltage to open, and the battery soaks up voltage spikes','Without the battery the generators would motor the engines from the GPU','The battery must be charged above 20 V before either inverter can start'],c:1,e:'To disconnect the avionics, the avionics power relays must be energised to OPEN. Without battery voltage the avionics could be exposed to the GPU\'s switch-on transients. Keeping the battery on line also helps filter spikes.'},
 {id:'elec-016',ch:'elec',lvl:1,q:'What kind of ground power unit should be used for engine starting?',a:['Any 12 V car-type booster connected through an adapter','About 1,000 A peak and about 300 A continuous at 28 V DC','About 1,000 A peak, but supplied only at 115 V AC and 400 Hz','About 300 A peak and about 100 A continuous, at 28 V DC regulated'],c:1,e:'Starting draws a very high current at first. The GPU should handle about 1,000 A peak and about 300 A continuous at 28 V DC.'},
 {id:'elec-017',ch:'elec',lvl:2,q:'How does starter current change during a normal start?',a:['It is near zero until 20 % N1, then climbs to about 1,100 A','It rises steadily to about 600 A until the generator comes on line','It peaks near 1,100 A and drops to about 300 A by about 20 % N1','It stays close to 250 A from the first rotation until the engine lights up'],c:2,e:'The initial current surge is very high, which explains the GPU and starter limits. By about 20 % N1 it has dropped to around 300 A, and as the engine self-accelerates it takes over from the starter. The generator is switched on once high idle has been selected.'},
 {id:'elec-018',ch:'elec',lvl:2,q:'What does holding the start/ignition switch in STARTER ONLY do?',a:['Latches the switch ON so it must be pulled out to release it','Arms the autoignition system for take-off and lights IGNITION ON','Motors the engine and fires the igniters continuously until it is released','Motors the engine with the starter but no ignition, for clearing or cooling'],c:3,e:'STARTER ONLY is spring-loaded. It motors the engine without ignition, for example to clear fuel or cool an engine after an aborted start. The ON position is lever-locked and gives both starter and ignition.'},
 {id:'elec-019',ch:'elec',lvl:1,q:'For how long may the battery be exposed to voltages above 30 V before it may be damaged?',a:['About two minutes','Indefinitely, because it is protected by the GCU','About thirty minutes','About ten seconds'],c:0,e:'Exposing the battery to more than 30 V for more than two minutes can damage it, which is one reason to check ground power voltage.'},
 {id:'elec-020',ch:'elec',lvl:3,q:'In cruise at FL250 the amber L DC GEN caution comes on. The left loadmeter reads zero and the right one has roughly doubled. What is the most likely situation, and what is powering the left generator bus now?',a:['Inverter failed; the left generator bus is now being fed with AC from the standby inverter','Left generator off line; the right one feeds the left bus via the isolation limiters','Both generators off line; all buses are now supplied by the battery alone for now','Left 325 A limiter blown; the left generator bus is now completely dead'],c:1,e:'With one generator off line, the paralleled bus system is still fed by the other generator through the isolation limiters. The doubled right load fits that. Keep the remaining generator within its limit and follow the QRH (for example one reset attempt).'},
 {id:'elec-021',ch:'elec',lvl:3,q:'You are at FL330 after a single generator failure, and the remaining loadmeter shows 95 %. What must you do?',a:['Keep resetting the failed generator until it comes back on line','Switch inverters, which halves the DC load on the generator','Cut the load to 88 % or less, or descend to FL310 or below','Nothing, since 100 % is allowed at every altitude'],c:2,e:'Above 31,000 ft the generator limit is 88 % because cooling is reduced. Either shed load or descend to where the 100 % limit applies, then follow the QRH.'},
 {id:'elec-022',ch:'elec',lvl:3,q:'Both L and R DC GEN cautions come on and neither generator will reset. What is the main concern, and what is the overall plan?',a:['No real change, because the inverters will now supply DC to all the buses automatically','Battery only, limited endurance: shed load, land as soon as practical (QRH)','Switch the battery OFF now to save its charge for the approach later','Both engines will flame out once the battery voltage has dropped below 24 V'],c:1,e:'The battery can power the systems, but only for a limited time. Load management and an early landing are the priorities, with QRH guidance for the detailed actions.'},
 {id:'elec-023',ch:'elec',lvl:3,q:'The red INVERTER warning comes on in cruise. What is the expected response?',a:['Switch the battery OFF and ON again to restart the inverter','Select the other inverter, check AC volts and frequency, then the QRH','Descend at once, because the engines need AC power to keep running normally','Turn both generators off and on again to reset the selected inverter'],c:1,e:'Only one inverter runs at a time, and the other is the standby. Selecting it normally restores AC to the avionics. Then confirm with the volt-frequency meter and finish the QRH items.'},
 {id:'elec-024',ch:'elec',lvl:3,q:'On the ground with air conditioning running, a late-serial B200 shows 82 % generator load at low idle. What does the ground-operation limit require?',a:['Turn the air conditioner off; the N1 setting makes no difference','Shut down the other engine so that one generator takes all of the load','Raise N1 to at least about 65 % (towards high idle) or reduce the load','Nothing, because the ground load table only applies above 85 % generator load'],c:2,e:'The ground table links generator load to minimum N1 to keep the generator cooled. At 80–85 % load, about 65 % N1 is required, with or without air conditioning.'},
 {id:'elec-025',ch:'elec',lvl:3,q:'On older NiCad-equipped aircraft a BATTERY CHG caution is fitted. What does it mean if it is still on long after an engine start?',a:['Excessive charge current; if it stays on with the battery OFF, land soon','The GPU is still plugged in and is overcharging the battery through the bus','One generator is off line and the battery is now carrying that bus','Battery fully charged; the caution can be ignored for the rest of the flight'],c:0,e:'After a battery start, a high recharge current turns the light on for a few minutes, which is normal and acts as a self-test. If it stays on, the charge rate is excessive. Later lead-acid aircraft use a battery ammeter instead of this caution.'},
 {id:'elec-026',ch:'elec',lvl:3,q:'In flight the avionics master control circuit breaker trips. What happens to the avionics buses?',a:['Only avionics bus No. 2 stays powered, from the right generator bus','They stay powered, because the avionics relays need power to open','They change over automatically to the hot battery bus','They lose power at once, because the avionics relays need power to close'],c:1,e:'The avionics master relays are fail-safe: they need power to open and disconnect the avionics. Losing their control power therefore leaves the avionics on, not off.'},
 {id:'elec-027',ch:'elec',lvl:1,q:'Where is the external power receptacle on the B200?',a:['Under the right wing, outboard of the nacelle','In the nose baggage area on the left side','Under the left wing, outboard of the engine nacelle','In the right main wheel well, aft of the strut'],c:0,e:'The ground power socket is on the underside of the right wing, outboard of the nacelle.'},
 {id:'elec-028',ch:'elec',lvl:2,q:'Where do the inverters get their DC input power from?',a:['From avionics buses 1 and 2, through the avionics master','From the external power relay whenever a GPU is plugged in and on','From the hot battery bus only, so they work with BAT switch off','Directly from the generator buses, No. 1 left and No. 2 right'],c:3,e:'The inverters are powered straight from the generator buses, each through its own limiter: No. 1 from the left and No. 2 from the right. A generator failure does not stop either inverter while the buses are paralleled.'},
 {id:'light-001',ch:'light',lvl:1,q:'Where are the landing and taxi lights fitted on the B200?',a:['One landing light in each wingtip','All of them under the fuselage belly','Landing lights in the wing leading edges and the taxi light on the tail','Two landing lights and a taxi light on the nose gear'],c:3,e:'Two sealed-beam landing lights are mounted on the nose gear, with the taxi light just below them. That is why they point with the nose-wheel and retract with the gear.'},
 {id:'light-002',ch:'light',lvl:2,q:'How do the threshold and aisle lights behave if their switch is left ON?',a:['They light when the door opens and go out once it is closed and locked','They stay on until the battery switch is turned OFF at the end of the shutdown','They flash in step with the beacon for as long as the door is open','They work only when at least one engine is running'],c:0,e:'The entry lights are switched through the door mechanism. With the switch ON they light when the door opens and go off once the door is closed and the handle locked.'},
 {id:'light-003',ch:'light',lvl:3,q:'Why should you check the baggage area light switch when securing the aircraft?',a:['It also powers the fire detection loop, which stays energised for as long as it is lit','It is on the hot battery bus with no auto shut-off, so it can drain the battery','It keeps the selected inverter running after the battery switch has been turned off','It is linked to the oxygen system and keeps the passenger valve armed'],c:1,e:'The baggage light is on the hot battery bus and has no automatic shut-off, so if it is left on it slowly drains the battery.'},
 {id:'light-004',ch:'light',lvl:2,q:'What happens to the cabin lighting when the passenger oxygen system starts to flow?',a:['The cabin lights switch off to save power for the oxygen controller','Only the reading lights flash to show where the masks are','Nothing; the cabin lighting is independent of the oxygen system','The cabin lights go to full bright, whatever their switch position'],c:3,e:'On these aircraft the passenger-oxygen pressure switch also forces the fluorescent, vestibule and baggage lights to full bright, so passengers can see the masks.'},
 {id:'light-005',ch:'light',lvl:2,q:'What is special about using the wing ice inspection lights on the ground?',a:['They work only when the gear is up, through the right squat switch','They can be powered only from a ground power unit on the ground','They run very hot, so avoid long periods of use on the ground','They work only when the inertial separators are extended'],c:2,e:'The ice lights on the nacelles light the wing leading edges. They run hot, so avoid prolonged use on the ground.'},
 {id:'light-006',ch:'light',lvl:1,q:'Where are the beacons on the B200?',a:['One on top of the fin and one under the fuselage','One on each engine nacelle, facing outboard and slightly up','Only one, in the tail cone just below the rudder','One on each wingtip, next to the strobes'],c:0,e:'There is a beacon on top of the fin and another on the lower fuselage just ahead of the main gear area. The strobes are in the wingtips and the tail cone.'},
 {id:'warn-001',ch:'warn',lvl:1,q:'What do the annunciator colours mean on the B200?',a:['Red and amber: memory items; green: a system has failed','No fixed meaning; the colours depend on the serial number','Red: advisory only; amber: warning needing action; green: caution','Red: immediate action; amber: attention needed; green: normal state'],c:3,e:'Red warnings are on the glareshield panel and call for immediate action. Amber cautions call for attention. Green advisories only report a functional state and have no master flasher.'},
 {id:'warn-002',ch:'warn',lvl:2,q:'You press the flashing MASTER WARNING light, but the fault is still there. What happens next?',a:['The flasher and the red annunciator both go out, and stay out until the next engine start','The flasher goes out, the red annunciator stays on, and a new warning re-triggers it','The fault is reset, and the system re-tests itself before lighting the annunciator again','The flasher keeps flashing until the fault has been cleared'],c:1,e:'Pressing the master flasher only resets the flasher. The red annunciator stays lit until the fault clears, and any new warning triggers the master flashers again.'},
 {id:'warn-003',ch:'warn',lvl:2,q:'Why is there no \'master advisory\' flasher for the green lights?',a:['Green lights show normal states that need no immediate attention','Green lights are not checked by the annunciator press-to-test switch','Green lights are too dim to be seen together with a flasher','Green advisories are shown only on the ground and are inhibited in flight'],c:0,e:'Advisories such as FUEL CROSSFEED or BL AIR OFF just tell you a system is in a given state. They go out only when that state ends and do not use the master caution or warning flashers.'},
 {id:'warn-004',ch:'warn',lvl:1,q:'What does the PRESS TO TEST button on the glareshield NOT test?',a:['The red warning annunciators on the glareshield','The green advisory annunciators on the subpanel','The amber MASTER CAUTION flashers on the glareshield','The amber NO TRANSFER lights on the fuel panel'],c:3,e:'The test lights the annunciators and all master flashers. The NO TRANSFER lights are not part of it, because they do not drive the master caution flashers.'},
 {id:'warn-005',ch:'warn',lvl:2,q:'The annunciators change to dim mode automatically only when several conditions are met. Which set is correct?',a:['Overhead flood lights on and the photocell sensing bright sunlight','Generator on line, MASTER PANEL on, floods off, pilot flight lights on, dark cockpit','Battery on, nav lights on, and the time of day set to night on the cockpit clock','Only while the pilot holds the PRESS TO TEST switch down in its DIM position at night'],c:1,e:'All the listed conditions must be met at once, including the photocell sensing a dark cockpit. Otherwise the system defaults to bright so that annunciators are never too dim in daylight.'},
 {id:'warn-006',ch:'warn',lvl:3,q:'On final approach with the gear down, the amber RVS NOT READY caution comes on. What is the meaning, and what should you do?',a:['Power levers are in beta; move them forward to the flight idle gate before touchdown','Prop levers not fully forward; move them to full forward for reversing','Reverse system has failed; plan the landing without using reverse at all','Gear not locked; go around and check the gear indications'],c:1,e:'Consistent reversing needs the propeller levers fully forward, and with the gear extended this caution warns if they are not. Setting the levers forward clears it.'},
 {id:'warn-007',ch:'warn',lvl:2,q:'When does the green L or R IGNITION ON advisory illuminate?',a:['Whenever an igniter has failed and needs to be replaced','Switch ON, or autoignition armed with torque below about 400 ft-lb','Whenever the autoignition switch is set to ARM, whatever the engine torque','Only during a start, while the starter is turning the engine over'],c:1,e:'The light shows that the igniters are powered. With autoignition armed, that happens only when torque drops below about 400 ft-lb, so in normal cruise the light should be out.'},
 {id:'warn-008',ch:'warn',lvl:1,q:'What triggers the red ALT WARN annunciator?',a:['Cabin pressure altitude above about 12,500 ft','Cabin differential above 6.5 psi','Aircraft pressure altitude above 31,000 ft with the cabin pressurised','An altitude preselect deviation of more than 1,000 ft'],c:0,e:'A pressure switch on the forward bulkhead closes at about 12,500 ft cabin altitude. It warns the crew that oxygen is needed.'},
 {id:'warn-009',ch:'warn',lvl:2,q:'On a typical late-serial B200, how is a chip-detector indication presented, and where is the detector?',a:['Amber CHIP DETECT caution; magnetic plug in the reduction gearbox','Green CHIP advisory; metal sensor in the firewall fuel filter bowl','Red CHIP DETECT warning; magnetic plug in the oil tank','Amber OIL caution; metal sensor in the accessory gearbox scavenge line'],c:0,e:'On later B200s the chip lights are amber cautions; older aircraft had them as red warnings. The magnetic detector sits at the bottom of the reduction gearbox, and a light means ferrous particles in the oil.'},
 {id:'warn-010',ch:'warn',lvl:3,q:'Autofeather is armed for an approach. On short final you reduce power and both green AUTOFEATHER lights go out. What does this tell you?',a:['One propeller has feathered and the other is about to follow it','A fault: the autofeather system has failed and the failure must be reported','Normal: arming needs high power-lever position and torque above 400 ft-lb','The autofeather switch has tripped back to OFF on its own'],c:2,e:'Autofeather is armed only when the power-lever switches are closed (high power) and torque is above about 400 ft-lb. Lights going out with low approach power are expected, and with a go-around at high power they come back on.'},
 {id:'warn-011',ch:'warn',lvl:3,q:'What does the green FUEL CROSSFEED advisory actually confirm?',a:['That both standby boost pumps are running and pressurised','That fuel is actually flowing from one wing system to the other','That the crossfeed valve has reached its fully open position','Only the switch selection, not the valve\'s actual position'],c:3,e:'The light is driven by the switch selection. To check that the valve really opened, use the crossfeed test before start, where both FUEL PRESS lights should go out.'},
 {id:'warn-012',ch:'warn',lvl:3,q:'The amber DUCT OVERTEMP caution comes on in cruise with automatic heating. What is the most likely cause and the general response?',a:['Ruptured bleed-air line: close both bleed-air valves at once and start a descent','Hot duct air (about 300°F), usually low airflow: cut heat or add airflow (QRH)','Air-conditioning compressor overheating: shut down the right engine and follow the QRH','Cabin altitude too high: put on oxygen masks and start a descent (QRH)'],c:1,e:'DUCT OVERTEMP warns of hot duct air, typically from low airflow with hot bleed air. Lowering the temperature demand or increasing the airflow, as directed by the QRH, normally clears it. A bleed-line rupture is shown by the red BL AIR FAIL instead.'},
 {id:'fuel-001',ch:'fuel',lvl:1,q:'What is the total usable fuel capacity of the B200 (main plus auxiliary systems)?',a:['600 US gal, about 4,020 lb at 6.7 lb/gal','430 US gal, about 2,881 lb at 6.7 lb/gal','544 US gal, about 3,644 lb at 6.7 lb/gal','386 US gal, about 2,586 lb at 6.7 lb/gal'],c:2,e:'The main systems hold 386 gal and the auxiliary tanks 158 gal, so 544 gal in total. At the standard 6.7 lb/gal that is about 3,644 lb.'},
 {id:'fuel-002',ch:'fuel',lvl:1,q:'How much usable fuel does each wing\'s main system hold, and how much does each auxiliary tank hold?',a:['193 gal main and 57 gal auxiliary per side','193 gal main and 79 gal auxiliary per side','158 gal main and 193 gal auxiliary per side','About 272 gal main and 79 gal auxiliary per side'],c:1,e:'Each side\'s main system holds 193 gal (386 gal total) and each auxiliary tank 79 gal (158 gal total).'},
 {id:'fuel-003',ch:'fuel',lvl:1,q:'What is the usable capacity of each nacelle tank?',a:['40 gal','About 79 gal','57 gal','25 gal'],c:2,e:'The nacelle tank holds 57 gal. The other main-system cells hold 40 + 13 gal (leading edge), 25 + 23 gal (box section) and 35 gal (integral wing), which adds up to 193 gal per side.'},
 {id:'fuel-004',ch:'fuel',lvl:2,q:'How does fuel from the outer wing reach the engine in the main system?',a:['An electric transfer pump in each wing cell pumps the fuel directly to the engine','Bleed air pressurises the wing cells and pushes fuel to the nacelle','The integral wing tank feeds the engine directly, bypassing the nacelle tank altogether','All main-system cells gravity-feed the nacelle tank, which feeds the engine'],c:3,e:'The main-system cells all gravity-feed into the nacelle tank, and the engine draws from there. A swing check valve stops fuel flowing back outboard while auxiliary transfer is running.'},
 {id:'fuel-005',ch:'fuel',lvl:2,q:'Why must the main tanks be full before any fuel goes into the auxiliary tanks?',a:['The aux filler caps are mechanically blocked until the main tanks are full','Aux fuel is burned first and only transfers into the mains; the system assumes full mains','The capacitance gauges cannot read aux fuel while the main tanks are part-full','The aux tanks would overflow into the cabin through the vents if the mains are not full'],c:1,e:'The design assumes full mains whenever aux fuel is carried, with aux fuel used first by automatic transfer. Loading aux fuel without full mains is not allowed.'},
 {id:'fuel-006',ch:'fuel',lvl:2,q:'How is auxiliary fuel moved into the nacelle tank?',a:['By gravity alone, through the crossfeed line and a swing check valve','By a jet pump using motive-flow fuel, controlled automatically','By an electric aux transfer pump that the pilot switches on','By bleed-air pressure applied to the aux bladder cell'],c:1,e:'Fuel from the boost pump passes through the motive-flow valve to a jet pump in the aux tank. The venturi suction draws aux fuel along into the nacelle tank. In AUTO the pilot does nothing.'},
 {id:'fuel-007',ch:'fuel',lvl:2,q:'After engine start, when should the amber NO TRANSFER light go out, assuming there is fuel in the aux tank?',a:['About 30–50 s after start, once the motive-flow valve opens','Only once airborne, when the squat switch enables the transfer system','After about 10 minutes, when the nacelle tank has room','Immediately when the condition lever reaches low idle'],c:0,e:'A 30–50 second delay keeps full fuel pressure available for the start. The motive-flow valve then opens, and once transfer-line pressure reaches about 4–6 psi the NO TRANSFER light goes out. Check this, and watch aux quantity fall early in the flight.'},
 {id:'fuel-008',ch:'fuel',lvl:3,q:'Aux fuel is used up normally and the float switch closes the motive-flow valve. What happens to the NO TRANSFER light?',a:['It comes on together with the MASTER CAUTION until reset','It flashes for about 60 seconds, then goes out by itself','It comes on steady, as a normal reminder that the aux tank is now empty','It stays out; it only lights with aux fuel left but not transferring'],c:3,e:'The NO TRANSFER light needs fuel in the aux tank and no transfer pressure. When the tank is empty there is nothing left to transfer, so the light stays out. A short delay prevents the valve cycling as fuel sloshes.'},
 {id:'fuel-009',ch:'fuel',lvl:3,q:'In cruise the right NO TRANSFER light comes on while the gauge, switched to AUX, still shows fuel in the right auxiliary tank. What is the likely cause and the action?',a:['Right engine-driven boost pump has failed; select crossfeed to feed the right engine','Transfer is working normally; the light is only a reminder to check the aux gauge','Aux tank is actually empty and the gauge is wrong; ignore the light for now','Motive-flow valve failed closed; select AUX TRANSFER to OVERRIDE and check aux falls'],c:3,e:'If the valve or its control fails, it closes and transfer stops. The OVERRIDE position powers the valve open directly. Confirm success by watching the aux quantity decrease.'},
 {id:'fuel-010',ch:'fuel',lvl:1,q:'At what fuel pressure does the red FUEL PRESS warning come on?',a:['Below about 25 psi','Below about 4–6 psi','Below about 10 ±1 psi','Below about 70 psi'],c:2,e:'The pressure switch downstream of the boost pump turns the light on below about 10 psi. Normal boost pressure is around 25–30 psi.'},
 {id:'fuel-011',ch:'fuel',lvl:2,q:'Which are the three jobs of the electric standby boost pump in each nacelle tank?',a:['Purge the nozzles, cool the engine oil, and feed the starter','Defuelling, refuelling pressure, and crossfeed only','Transfer aux fuel, heat the fuel for the FCU, and prime the engine for start','Back up the engine boost pump, avgas above 20,000 ft, and crossfeed'],c:3,e:'The standby pump backs up the engine-driven boost pump, prevents vapour problems with avgas at high altitude, and supplies the pressure for crossfeed. If it fails, crossfeed from that side is impossible.'},
 {id:'fuel-012',ch:'fuel',lvl:3,q:'In cruise the red L FUEL PRESS warning comes on, and the left engine instruments look normal. What is the likely cause, the action, and the limitation?',a:['Left engine boost pump failed: left standby pump ON; 10-hour limit with the light on','Crossfeed valve has opened by itself: select crossfeed OFF and monitor','Left firewall filter is bypassing: no action is needed and no limit applies','Left high-pressure engine pump has failed: shut the left engine down immediately (QRH)'],c:0,e:'Low-pressure-side failure is the usual cause. The standby pump restores inlet pressure to the HP pump and the light goes out. Time with the light on is capped at 10 hours because the HP pump may be damaged by cavitation. Follow the QRH.'},
 {id:'fuel-013',ch:'fuel',lvl:2,q:'On a typical late-serial B200, where do the standby boost pumps and firewall shut-off valves get electrical power?',a:['Only the hot battery bus, for both the pumps and the firewall valves','Avionics buses 1 and 2, through the normally closed avionics relays','Dual-fed buses 3 (L) and 4 (R); firewall valves also hot battery bus','Dual-fed buses 1 (L) and 2 (R); pumps also from the hot battery bus'],c:2,e:'Standby pumps and firewall valves are on buses 3 and 4. The firewall valves also have a hot battery bus feed, so they can be closed whatever the battery switch position. Earlier aircraft also had the pumps on the hot battery bus.'},
 {id:'fuel-014',ch:'fuel',lvl:2,q:'What does selecting CROSSFEED (left or right) do automatically?',a:['Opens the valve, runs the supplying standby pump, closes the receiver\'s motive valve','Opens both firewall valves and runs both standby pumps continuously','Transfers aux fuel from the supplying wing\'s aux tank into the other wing\'s aux tank','Closes the supplying firewall valve and opens the crossfeed valve'],c:0,e:'Crossfeed is a one-switch action. The supplying side\'s standby pump provides pressure, and aux transfer on the receiving side stops; if it still has aux fuel, its NO TRANSFER light comes on. The standby pump switches should be OFF when crossfeeding.'},
 {id:'fuel-015',ch:'fuel',lvl:3,q:'Single-engine with the left engine shut down, you want to feed the right engine from the left wing, but the LEFT standby pump is inoperative. What can you do?',a:['Turn on the right standby pump to suck fuel from the left wing','Not possible: crossfeed needs the supplying side\'s standby pump','Open the left firewall valve and fuel will gravity-feed across','It still works, because the right engine\'s pumps draw fuel across'],c:1,e:'Crossfeed depends on the standby pump on the supplying side. If it is dead, you can only crossfeed from the side with a working standby pump.'},
 {id:'fuel-016',ch:'fuel',lvl:3,q:'After an engine shutdown that was NOT caused by fire or a fuel leak, you want to use the dead side\'s auxiliary fuel by crossfeed. What must be set on the dead side?',a:['AUX TRANSFER OFF and condition lever at low idle','AUX TRANSFER in OVERRIDE and firewall valve closed','Standby pump ON, crossfeed OFF, firewall valve closed','AUX TRANSFER in AUTO and firewall valve open'],c:3,e:'Motive flow for aux transfer is taken from downstream of the firewall valve and filter, so with the valve closed no aux fuel can reach the nacelle tank. Open the dead side\'s firewall valve and leave AUX TRANSFER in AUTO, but never reopen the valve after a fire or fuel leak.'},
 {id:'fuel-017',ch:'fuel',lvl:3,q:'During the crossfeed check before start, what shows that the crossfeed valve has actually opened?',a:['Both fuel gauges start to change','The green FUEL CROSSFEED advisory comes on','Both red FUEL PRESS lights go out','Both NO TRANSFER lights come on'],c:2,e:'With the engines stopped, both FUEL PRESS lights are on. When crossfeed is selected, the supplying standby pump pressurises both sides through the open valve and both lights go out. The green advisory only shows the switch position.'},
 {id:'fuel-018',ch:'fuel',lvl:1,q:'Which fuels are approved as normal (recommended) fuels for the B200?',a:['Jet A and Jet A-1 only, and never mixed with any other grade of fuel','Jet A, Jet A-1, Jet B, JP-4 and JP-5, mixed in any ratio','Jet A and JP-8 only; Jet B is prohibited','Jet A-1 and 100LL, mixed in any ratio'],c:1,e:'The kerosene-type and wide-cut jet fuels listed are approved and may be mixed. Aviation gasoline grades are emergency fuels only.'},
 {id:'fuel-019',ch:'fuel',lvl:2,q:'What operating penalty applies when aviation gasoline is used as an emergency fuel?',a:['150 h limit per overhaul; crossfeed is prohibited at all altitudes','150 h limit per overhaul; above 20,000 ft both standby pumps needed','10 h limit in total, and only for ground running and taxi operations','No penalty, provided it is mixed at least 50/50 with Jet A beforehand'],c:1,e:'Avgas is allowed only as an emergency fuel. Each engine\'s avgas time counts against a 150-hour limit, and at high altitude the standby pumps are needed to prevent vapour lock.'},
 {id:'fuel-020',ch:'fuel',lvl:3,q:'Climbing through FL220 on avgas, both FUEL PRESS lights flicker and torque and fuel flow fluctuate. What is happening, and what fixes it?',a:['The anti-icing additive is too strong; select crossfeed to dilute it','Both engine-driven boost pumps have failed; shut down one engine','The fuel heaters are overheating the fuel; reduce power to idle','Avgas vaporising from low inlet pressure; turn on both standby pumps'],c:3,e:'Avgas vaporises more easily at altitude. Flickering FUEL PRESS lights are the first sign of low pressure, and switching on the standby pumps pressurises the supply and stops the problem.'},
 {id:'fuel-021',ch:'fuel',lvl:1,q:'If anti-icing additive is added to the fuel, what concentration by volume is required?',a:['Exactly 0.5 %','Between 1.0 % and 1.5 % by volume','Any amount up to 5 %','Between 0.10 % and 0.15 %'],c:3,e:'The additive must be blended to 0.10–0.15 % by volume. Too little does not protect, and too much can damage the fuel cells. Check whether the supplier has already blended it.'},
 {id:'fuel-022',ch:'fuel',lvl:2,q:'Why is the fuel heated before it reaches the fuel control unit, and how?',a:['To prevent vapour lock; the boost pump warms it to 120°F','To prevent FCU icing; an oil-to-fuel heat exchanger keeps it at 70–90°F','To raise fuel density; an electric heater keeps it at about 150°F','To reduce ITT; bleed air heats the fuel to about 100°F'],c:1,e:'Engine oil warms the fuel through a heat exchanger, regulated thermostatically. If oil is too cold and fuel very cold, the heater may not be enough, which is when anti-icing additive is needed according to the chart.'},
 {id:'fuel-023',ch:'fuel',lvl:1,q:'What is the maximum allowable fuel imbalance between the two wing systems?',a:['About 1,500 lb','1,000 lb','265 lb','500 lb'],c:1,e:'The basic limit is 1,000 lb. Autopilot supplements may impose their own limits.'},
 {id:'fuel-024',ch:'fuel',lvl:1,q:'Below what quantity on either fuel gauge is take-off prohibited?',a:['165 lb','500 lb','1,000 lb','265 lb'],c:3,e:'Take-off is not allowed if either gauge shows less than 265 lb or is in the yellow arc.'},
 {id:'fuel-025',ch:'fuel',lvl:2,q:'How is fuel quantity measured and shown?',a:['Float transmitters, read in gallons, with a separate gauge for every tank','Dipsticks before flight, with fuel flow integration in flight','Capacitance probes, read in pounds, density-corrected; MAIN/AUX selector','Fuel-used totaliser only, set by the pilot before each flight'],c:2,e:'Capacitance probes in the cells measure the fuel. The readout is in pounds and allows for temperature and density changes. Hold the selector at AUX to read the auxiliary tank.'},
 {id:'fuel-026',ch:'fuel',lvl:2,q:'Why does each wing have two different fuel vents under the wing?',a:['One is for pressure refuelling and the other for defuelling','One is recessed and ice-resistant, the other protrudes and is heated','One vents the auxiliary tank and the other vents the main fuel system','Both are pressure-relief valves that protect the bladder cells'],c:1,e:'Two different vents give redundancy in icing: one by its aerodynamic shape, the other by heating. Switch on FUEL VENT heat in icing conditions.'},
 {id:'fuel-027',ch:'fuel',lvl:3,q:'After shutdown you notice a brief rise in N1 and ITT. On aircraft with the fuel purge system, why is that normal?',a:['The starter re-engages briefly to help cool the turbine section','The FCU spools the engine up briefly to cool the bearings','The fuel heater dumps warm fuel back into the engine manifolds at shutdown','Purge air blows leftover manifold fuel into the combustor, where it burns'],c:3,e:'Air stored in a purge tank blows the remaining manifold fuel into the still-burning combustor, which gives a small, normal N1 and ITT rise.'},
 {id:'power-001',ch:'power',lvl:1,q:'How is the PT6A-42 compressor built?',a:['Two centrifugal stages only','Four axial stages','Three axial stages followed by one centrifugal stage','One axial stage followed by two centrifugal stages in series'],c:2,e:'The gas generator compressor has three axial stages and then one centrifugal stage, driven by a single-stage compressor turbine.'},
 {id:'power-002',ch:'power',lvl:1,q:'Which turbines are in the PT6A-42, and what does each drive?',a:['2-stage compressor turbine (propeller); 1-stage power turbine (compressor, accessories)','1-stage compressor turbine (compressor); 1-stage power turbine (propeller), no gearbox','1-stage compressor turbine (compressor, accessories); 2-stage power turbine (propeller)','One 3-stage turbine driving compressor and propeller on a common shaft'],c:2,e:'The PT6 is a free-turbine engine. The compressor turbine and the power turbine turn on separate shafts, rotating in opposite directions.'},
 {id:'power-003',ch:'power',lvl:2,q:'What does \'free turbine\' mean in practical terms for the pilot?',a:['The turbine blades need no cooling air because they turn freely','A clutch lets the propeller be disconnected from the engine in flight for windmilling','The engine can run without a fuel control once it is started','The power turbine is linked to the gas generator only by gas flow, not by a shaft'],c:3,e:'Because the power turbine is only coupled aerodynamically, the prop governor holds Np while the power lever sets N1 and fuel. This is also why the starter turns only the gas generator, not the propeller.'},
 {id:'power-004',ch:'power',lvl:1,q:'What is in the PT6A combustion section?',a:['Six can-type combustors with one igniter in each can','An annular chamber with 14 nozzles and two igniters','An annular chamber with 7 nozzles and a single igniter','A reverse-flow can chamber with 20 nozzles and two igniters'],c:1,e:'Fuel arrives through 14 simplex nozzles on two manifolds (primary and secondary). Two high-energy igniter plugs light the mixture.'},
 {id:'power-005',ch:'power',lvl:2,q:'What do the compressor bleed valves do, and when are they closed?',a:['Vent surplus air to prevent stall at low N1; closed above about 90 % N1','Cool the turbine section; open at all times with the engine running','Supply pressurisation bleed air; closed on the ground by the squat switch','Limit torque at high power; open above about 90 % N1'],c:0,e:'At low N1 the axial stages deliver more air than the centrifugal stage can accept, so the bleed valves vent the excess. At high power they close for full efficiency.'},
 {id:'power-006',ch:'power',lvl:3,q:'In stable cruise you notice ITT higher and torque lower than normal for the power setting, with N1 unchanged. Which engine fault fits best?',a:['A torque limiter fault','A compressor bleed valve stuck closed at low N1','A compressor bleed valve stuck open','A failed propeller governor'],c:2,e:'A bleed valve stuck open wastes compressed air, so torque drops and ITT rises at the same N1. A valve stuck closed at low N1 would cause compressor stall instead.'},
 {id:'power-007',ch:'power',lvl:2,q:'In the forward thrust range, how does the power lever control the engine?',a:['It sets the FCU\'s N1 governor, which meters fuel to hold that N1','It sets the propeller blade angle directly through the beta valve and linkage','It opens and closes the fuel cut-off valve in the FCU','It resets the propeller governor to a new rpm datum'],c:0,e:'In the alpha range the power lever selects an N1 speed. The pneumatic FCU then adjusts fuel flow to reach and hold it. Propeller rpm is the job of the propeller lever and governor.'},
 {id:'power-008',ch:'power',lvl:1,q:'What are the condition lever positions and the related N1 on a typical late-serial B200?',a:['FEATHER, LOW IDLE (about 56 % N1 min) and TAKEOFF (about 100 % N1)','CUTOFF, LOW IDLE (about 52 % N1 min), HIGH IDLE (about 61 %)','OFF, START (about 20 % N1) and RUN (about 70 % N1)','CUTOFF, LOW IDLE (about 61 % N1 min), HIGH IDLE (about 70 %)'],c:3,e:'The condition lever opens and closes the FCU cut-off valve and sets the idle schedule. Low idle minimum is about 61 % on later 4-blade B200s (56 % on earlier B200s, 52 % on the -41) and high idle is about 70 %.'},
 {id:'power-009',ch:'power',lvl:2,q:'Why do pilots often select HIGH IDLE for ground reversing or high electrical loads on the ground?',a:['It sets the propeller governors to 2,000 rpm automatically','About 70 % N1 gives faster reverse spool-up and more generator capacity','It feathers the propellers so they stop dragging the aircraft forward on the ground','It turns autoignition on for the whole ground operation'],c:1,e:'High idle raises the minimum N1. That shortens spool-up time when reversing and gives enough N1 for high generator loads or air conditioning.'},
 {id:'power-010',ch:'power',lvl:3,q:'During start, N1 surges noticeably at around 40 %. Why?',a:['The secondary nozzles have started flowing','The compressor bleed valves have closed','The starter has just dropped off line','The propeller has just come off the feather stops'],c:0,e:'Starts begin on the primary nozzles only, which keeps the start cooler. At around 40 % N1 the flow divider brings in the secondary nozzles, and a small N1 surge is normal.'},
 {id:'power-011',ch:'power',lvl:2,q:'What happens if the engine-driven high-pressure fuel pump fails in flight?',a:['Nothing, because the standby boost pump takes over','The engine keeps running at idle on gravity feed','The FCU reverts to a manual mode with a fixed fuel flow','Flameout, because the minimum pressurising valve closes'],c:3,e:'The standby pump only replaces the low-pressure boost pump. Without the HP pump (about 800 psi), the minimum pressurising valve shuts and combustion stops.'},
 {id:'power-012',ch:'power',lvl:1,q:'How is ITT measured on the PT6A-42?',a:['An infrared sensor looking at the compressor turbine disk','Four probes in front of the compressor, corrected for airspeed and altitude','Eight parallel thermocouples between the compressor and power turbines','One thermocouple in each exhaust stack, averaged by the gauge'],c:2,e:'The eight probes are averaged electrically, and a trimmer calibrates the reading. ITT is the main temperature limit you watch at start and at high power.'},
 {id:'power-013',ch:'power',lvl:1,q:'What is the maximum ITT for take-off and maximum continuous power on the PT6A-42?',a:['750°C','850°C','1,000°C','800°C'],c:3,e:'The take-off and max continuous ITT limit is 800°C, and the recommended normal-cruise ITT is 770°C. The older -41 was limited to 750°C.'},
 {id:'power-014',ch:'power',lvl:1,q:'What is the ITT limit during starting?',a:['800°C with no time limit','1,100°C for no more than 10 seconds','850°C for no more than 20 seconds','1,000°C for no more than 5 seconds'],c:3,e:'The starting limit is shown by a dashed red line at 1,000°C and is limited to 5 seconds. Anything beyond is an overtemperature that needs maintenance action.'},
 {id:'power-015',ch:'power',lvl:1,q:'What is the maximum torque for take-off and maximum continuous, and the transient limit?',a:['2,750 ft-lb; transient 3,000 ft-lb for no more than 5 s','2,000 ft-lb; transient 2,230 ft-lb for no more than 20 s','1,800 ft-lb; transient 2,230 ft-lb for no more than 10 s','2,230 ft-lb; transient 2,750 ft-lb for no more than 5 s'],c:3,e:'Maximum steady torque is 2,230 ft-lb. A transient to 2,750 ft-lb is allowed for 5 seconds at most.'},
 {id:'power-016',ch:'power',lvl:1,q:'What are the N1 limits on the PT6A-42?',a:['104.0 % maximum; 106.0 % transient for 10 s','98.0 % maximum; 101.5 % transient for 20 s','101.5 % maximum; 102.6 % transient for 10 s','100.0 % maximum; 101.5 % transient for 5 s'],c:2,e:'100 % N1 is 37,500 rpm. The steady limit is 101.5 %, with a 10-second allowance up to 102.6 %.'},
 {id:'power-017',ch:'power',lvl:1,q:'What are the propeller rpm limits?',a:['1,900 normal, 2,000 reverse, 2,120 transient for 10 s','2,200 normal, 2,000 reverse, 2,300 transient for 5 s','2,120 normal, 2,200 reverse, no transient allowed','2,000 normal, 1,900 reverse, 2,200 transient for 5 s'],c:3,e:'Np is limited to 2,000 rpm in all normal conditions and 1,900 rpm in reverse. A 2,200 rpm transient is allowed for 5 seconds.'},
 {id:'power-018',ch:'power',lvl:2,q:'Why is torque limited to 1,100 ft-lb below 1,600 propeller rpm?',a:['ITT always rises above 800°C below 1,600 propeller rpm','High torque at low rpm overloads the propeller and gearbox','Autofeather arms automatically below 1,600 propeller rpm in flight','The generator overheats when propeller rpm is that low'],c:1,e:'The 2,230 ft-lb limit applies only from 1,600 to 2,000 rpm. Below 1,600 rpm the limit drops to 1,100 ft-lb to protect the drive train.'},
 {id:'power-019',ch:'power',lvl:3,q:'The engine has a torque limiter that acts at about 2,368–2,447 ft-lb. Does it stop the pilot exceeding the 2,230 ft-lb limit?',a:['No; it only protects the gearbox, so 2,230 ft-lb is up to the pilot','Yes; it holds torque at exactly 2,230 ft-lb in all flight conditions','Yes, but only while the autofeather system is armed','No; it limits ITT rather than torque, so it is not relevant'],c:0,e:'The limiter reduces fuel only above its setting, which is higher than the certified maximum. Staying at or below 2,230 ft-lb is the pilot\'s responsibility.'},
 {id:'power-020',ch:'power',lvl:1,q:'What is the normal (green) oil pressure range on a typical late-serial B200, and what is the minimum?',a:['60–100 psi (40–100 at or above 21,000 ft); minimum 40 psi','100–135 psi (85–135 at or above 21,000 ft); minimum 60 psi','135–200 psi (100–200 above 21,000 ft); minimum 100 psi','85–135 psi (60–135 at or above 21,000 ft); minimum 85 psi'],c:1,e:'The normal band is 100–135 psi, extended down to 85 psi at 21,000 ft and above (the yellow/green band). Below 60 psi is unsafe.'},
 {id:'power-021',ch:'power',lvl:3,q:'In cruise one engine\'s oil pressure settles at 70 psi with oil temperature normal. What does the guidance say?',a:['Unsafe: shut the engine down at once and land at the nearest suitable airport','Low: add power to raise oil pressure back into the green band','Undesirable: finish the flight at ≤1,100 ft-lb, monitor, follow the QRH','Normal above 21,000 ft: no action is needed if temperature is normal'],c:2,e:'60–85 psi is tolerated only to finish the flight at reduced power (≤1,100 ft-lb). Below 60 psi you must either shut down or land at the nearest suitable airport using minimum power.'},
 {id:'power-022',ch:'power',lvl:1,q:'What are the oil temperature limits?',a:['0 to 99°C; up to 110°C for 5 minutes','−40 to 85°C; no exceedance allowed','−20 to 104°C; up to 120°C for 2 minutes','−40 to 99°C; up to 104°C for 10 minutes'],c:3,e:'Normal maximum is 99°C, with a 10-minute allowance up to 104°C. A minimum of 55°C is recommended for proper fuel-heater function at take-off power.'},
 {id:'power-023',ch:'power',lvl:2,q:'Which statement about the PT6A oil system is correct?',a:['Wet sump in the accessory gearbox; it serves only the engine bearings and gears','It uses aircraft hydraulic fluid shared with the landing gear','Dry sump with an integral tank; it also serves prop governor and torquemeter','Dry sump with a tank in the wing; prop oil is a separate system'],c:2,e:'The oil tank is built into the engine, with one pressure pump and several scavenge pumps. Oil lubricates the engine and also powers the propeller control, torquemeter and torque limiter, and warms the fuel.'},
 {id:'power-024',ch:'power',lvl:2,q:'In the constant-speed range, what happens inside the propeller governor during an overspeed?',a:['Flyweights win, oil drains from the hub, and blade angle increases','The fuel topping governor feathers the propeller to stop the overspeed','The overspeed governor shuts the engine down through the FCU','Speeder spring wins, oil is added to the hub, and blade angle decreases'],c:0,e:'Oil pressure drives the blades to finer pitch; counterweights and the feathering spring drive them coarser. In an overspeed the flyweights lift the pilot valve, oil drains, and pitch increases.'},
 {id:'power-025',ch:'power',lvl:2,q:'What forces feather the propeller, and what force unfeathers it?',a:['Centrifugal twisting moment alone moves the blades both ways','Governor oil pressure feathers it; counterweights and a spring unfeather it','Counterweights and a spring feather it; governor oil pressure unfeathers it','An electric motor feathers it; air loads unfeather it when windmilling'],c:2,e:'With no oil pressure the blades go to feather. That is why loss of oil pressure, or the autofeather dump valve, feathers the propeller, and why the engine must be running to unfeather.'},
 {id:'power-026',ch:'power',lvl:2,q:'What is the overspeed governor setting on a typical late-serial B200, and what does the PROP GOV TEST switch do?',a:['About 2,000 rpm; the test feathers the propeller briefly','About 2,200 rpm; the test checks the autofeather dump valve circuit','About 1,900 rpm; the test checks the synchrophaser range','About 2,120 rpm; the test lowers it to about 1,830–1,910 rpm'],c:3,e:'The overspeed governor is a backup set above the primary governor\'s 2,000 rpm. Earlier aircraft used 2,080 rpm (104 %). The test switch lowers its setting so you can confirm it limits rpm during the run-up.'},
 {id:'power-027',ch:'power',lvl:3,q:'In cruise one propeller rises above 2,000 rpm and stabilises at about 2,100 rpm. Torque is 1,700 ft-lb. What has happened, and may you continue?',a:['Fuel topping governor working normally; no restriction applies to further flight','Propeller has entered beta; pull the power lever back to idle','Primary governor failed; continue up to 2,120 rpm with torque ≤1,800 ft-lb','Both governors failed; shut the engine down at once and land'],c:2,e:'A steady overspeed above 2,000 rpm means the primary governor has failed. Up to the overspeed governor setting (2,120 rpm on later aircraft) you may continue with torque ≤1,800 ft-lb. Steady speeds above that mean both governors have failed, which is unapproved; follow the QRH.'},
 {id:'power-028',ch:'power',lvl:2,q:'What does the fuel topping (power turbine) governor do, and what is its weakness?',a:['Cuts fuel at about 106 % of selected rpm; lost if the primary governor fails','Limits ITT to 800°C and works entirely independently of the propeller governor','Feathers the propeller automatically if rpm reaches 2,200','Controls N1 at idle only and has no effect in flight'],c:0,e:'It is mainly protection against a stuck propeller: cutting fuel reduces power and rpm. Because it uses the primary governor\'s flyweights, a primary governor failure takes it out too, and the overspeed governor provides the backup.'},
 {id:'power-029',ch:'power',lvl:2,q:'What happens when the power lever is moved from idle into full reverse?',a:['Blade angle goes to feather, N1 drops to low idle, topping governor is switched off','Blade angle goes negative, N1 stays at idle, propeller governor resets to 1,600 rpm','Blade angle stays fixed, N1 rises to 101.5 %, overspeed governor resets to 2,200 rpm','Blade angle goes negative, N1 rises to about 83 %, topping governor resets to about 95 %'],c:3,e:'Behind the gate the power lever controls blade angle through the beta valve and follow-up linkage, and through the cam box it asks for more N1. Lowering the topping governor setting keeps prop rpm below about 1,900 so the governor does not interfere.'},
 {id:'power-030',ch:'power',lvl:1,q:'What are the limits for maximum reverse on the PT6A-42?',a:['5 minutes; ITT 800°C, N1 101.5 %, 2,000 rpm','1 minute; ITT 750°C, N1 88 %, 1,900 rpm','30 seconds; ITT 850°C, N1 95 %, 2,200 rpm','No time limit; ITT 770°C, N1 88 %, 2,000 rpm'],c:1,e:'Reverse is time-limited to one minute, with lower ITT and N1 limits than in forward thrust and a prop limit of 1,900 rpm.'},
 {id:'power-031',ch:'power',lvl:2,q:'Why must the power levers never be lifted into ground fine or reverse when the engine is stopped or the propeller is feathered?',a:['Without oil pressure the blades can\'t follow and the linkage is damaged','It would engage the starter and begin to turn the engine over','It would close both firewall valves through the power-lever cam box and stop the fuel','It would arm the fire extinguisher squibs on both engines'],c:0,e:'Without governor oil pressure the blades cannot move to the commanded angle. Forcing the levers over the gates then strains and damages the beta and reverse linkage.'},
 {id:'power-032',ch:'power',lvl:2,q:'Why must the propeller levers be fully forward before you use reverse after landing?',a:['It raises the overspeed governor setting for the landing roll','Reversing is consistent only with the governor at its maximum setting','It feathers the propellers once the aircraft has slowed down','Reverse is mechanically locked out unless the levers are at 2,000 rpm'],c:1,e:'With the levers full forward, the governor stays out of the way and the power lever alone sets blade angle and N1 in reverse. The amber RVS NOT READY reminds you if they are not.'},
 {id:'power-033',ch:'power',lvl:1,q:'With AUTOIGNITION armed, when do the igniters fire?',a:['When torque falls to about 400 ft-lb or less','Continuously, whenever the switch is at the ARM position','Only when N1 falls below about 50 %','Only when the engine fire warning comes on'],c:0,e:'A torque switch powers the igniters if torque falls below about 400 ft-lb. That gives an automatic relight if a brief fuel or air interruption causes a flameout. Arm it for take-off and all flight phases, and turn it off after landing.'},
 {id:'power-034',ch:'power',lvl:2,q:'What must all be true for the green AUTOFEATHER lights to show the system armed?',a:['Switch at ARM, gear up, and both propellers at 2,000 rpm','Switch at TEST, power levers at idle, and both torques below about 200 ft-lb, gear down','Switch at ARM, power levers above about 90 % N1, both torques above 400 ft-lb','Switch at ARM, flaps at APPROACH, and both N1 above 70 %'],c:2,e:'Arming needs the switch, the power-lever microswitches (high power) and the torque switches. That is why the lights come on during the take-off roll and go out when you reduce power.'},
 {id:'power-035',ch:'power',lvl:3,q:'Shortly after lift-off the right engine fails with autofeather armed. Describe the autofeather sequence.',a:['Left light goes out at 400 ft-lb (disarmed); right prop feathers at 200 ft-lb','Both propellers feather together once either torque falls below 400 ft-lb','Right prop feathers at 400 ft-lb, then the left light goes out at about 200 ft-lb','Nothing happens until the pilot presses the right AUTOFEATHER light'],c:0,e:'The 400 ft-lb switch disarms the opposite engine so only one prop can autofeather. The 200 ft-lb switch then dumps the failed side\'s oil and the counterweights and springs feather it.'},
 {id:'power-036',ch:'power',lvl:3,q:'During the autofeather test (switch at TEST, both engines at about 500 ft-lb), you reduce the left power lever but the left propeller never starts to cycle and its light stays on. The condition levers are at HIGH IDLE. What is the most likely reason?',a:['The left dump valve has definitely failed and the propeller must be replaced','The test works only with the switch at ARM, not TEST','The right engine must be shut down before the left can cycle','At high idle torque may not fall below 200 ft-lb; use LOW IDLE and repeat'],c:3,e:'In the test the opposite light should go out near 400 ft-lb and the reduced engine\'s light near 200 ft-lb, then flash as the prop cycles. With the condition lever at high idle, torque may stay above 200 ft-lb, so the test cannot complete.'},
 {id:'power-037',ch:'power',lvl:2,q:'Why can\'t a feathered B200 propeller be unfeathered on a stopped engine?',a:['Feathered blades can only be moved by hand with the start locks removed','There is no unfeathering pump; governor oil pressure from a turning engine is needed','The propeller lever must first be moved into the reverse range to release the stop','An electric lock holds the blades in feather until the battery switch is turned on'],c:1,e:'Unfeathering uses governor pump oil pressure, so the engine must be turning. On a normal start, with the prop levers forward, the blades come off feather to the low-pitch stop as oil pressure builds.'},
 {id:'power-038',ch:'power',lvl:3,q:'Without autofeather armed, an engine flames out in cruise. Will its propeller feather by itself?',a:['No; windmilling keeps the governor supplied, so feather it with the lever','Yes; the fuel topping governor sends it to the feather position automatically','Only if the condition lever was left at low idle when it failed','Yes; the counterweights feather it as soon as combustion stops'],c:0,e:'A windmilling prop keeps the governor pump turning and the governor keeps trying to hold rpm by fining the pitch, which causes a lot of drag. Feathering needs the propeller lever moved to FEATHER (or autofeather).'},
 {id:'power-039',ch:'power',lvl:3,q:'During a battery start, ITT climbs very quickly towards the 1,000°C start limit while N1 is rising only slowly. What is the right response?',a:['Turn autoignition on and continue the start with the starter engaged','Condition lever to cut-off, keep motoring to cool within starter limits, then investigate','Continue the start; ITT may stay above 1,000°C for up to 20 seconds','Advance the power lever to increase the airflow through the engine, then continue the start'],c:1,e:'A hot start usually comes from too little airflow for the fuel being burned, often because of a weak battery. Cutting fuel and continuing to motor brings ITT down. The 1,000°C/5 s limit must not be exceeded.'},
 {id:'power-040',ch:'power',lvl:3,q:'After light-up, N1 stops increasing well below idle while ITT keeps slowly rising. What is this, and what do you do?',a:['Propeller governor fault: move the propeller lever to high rpm','Hot start: add power with the power lever to accelerate the engine','Normal start: the secondary nozzles will cut in shortly and N1 will then recover','Hung start: cut off fuel and motor to clear and cool, within starter limits'],c:3,e:'In a hung start N1 stagnates below idle and ITT creeps up because the engine cannot accelerate. Continuing risks an overtemperature, so abort and motor the engine to purge fuel and cool it.'},
 {id:'power-041',ch:'power',lvl:2,q:'Which items are driven by the engine accessory gearbox at the rear of the PT6?',a:['Overspeed governor, starter-generator, and the vacuum pump','FCU and HP pump, N1 tach, starter-generator, boost pump, A/C compressor (right)','Prop tach, torquemeter pump, and the hydraulic gear pump','Propeller governor, overspeed governor, prop tachometer and the A/C compressor (right)'],c:1,e:'The accessory gearbox is driven by the compressor turbine (N1 side). The prop governor, overspeed governor and prop tach are on the reduction gearbox, which is driven by the power turbine.'},
 {id:'power-042',ch:'power',lvl:2,q:'How does the Type II synchrophaser match propeller rpm?',a:['It briefly feathers one propeller to re-phase the blades','It adjusts both engines\' fuel flow through the FCUs','It trims the slower propeller up; it can\'t go below the lever setting','It trims the faster propeller down to match the slower one, within a limited range'],c:2,e:'Because it can only raise the slower propeller to match, a failure cannot pull rpm below what the levers select. If sync is lost, switch it off, synchronise manually and switch it on again.'},
 {id:'fire-001',ch:'fire',lvl:1,q:'On a typical late-serial B200, what kind of engine fire detector is fitted?',a:['A single thermocouple next to each exhaust stack','A temperature-sensing cable in each nacelle','Smoke detectors in each main wheel well','Three photo-electric flame detectors per engine'],c:1,e:'Later B200s have a temperature-sensing cable loop for each engine. Earlier aircraft used three photo-electric detectors per engine through an amplifier, and those could occasionally be triggered by sunlight.'},
 {id:'fire-002',ch:'fire',lvl:3,q:'During the pre-flight fire detection test on a later B200, the R ENG FIRE warning does not come on in the DET R position, although the left side tests normally. What does this mean for dispatch?',a:['Possible loop or wiring fault on the right; a failed test is a no-go until fixed','Retest after engine start, since the cable responds only when hot','Acceptable, because the left loop also covers the right nacelle','Acceptable, because the right extinguisher bottle still tests normally on the OK light'],c:0,e:'A lit warning in the test position proves the cable and wiring are intact. If a position fails, the system cannot be relied on and the aircraft should not be dispatched until the fault is corrected (or the MEL allows otherwise).'},
 {id:'fire-003',ch:'fire',lvl:1,q:'What agent is in the optional engine fire extinguishers, and how many shots does each engine have?',a:['Halon 1211; two bottles per engine fired one after the other','Halon 1301 (CBrF3) with nitrogen; one bottle, one shot per engine','CO2 with nitrogen; one bottle with two shots per engine','Dry powder; one shared bottle that can be aimed at either engine with a selector'],c:1,e:'Each nacelle has its own bottle, discharged through three spray bars when its squib fires. A bottle can be used once and cannot be routed to the other engine.'},
 {id:'fire-004',ch:'fire',lvl:2,q:'Why can the engine fire extinguishers be discharged even when the aircraft is parked with the battery switch OFF?',a:['They have their own batteries in the wheel wells','Their squib circuits are on the hot battery bus','They are fired by stored bleed-air pressure','They are fired mechanically by a pull cable'],c:1,e:'The extinguishing system is on the hot battery bus, so it is always available. The detection system, on the other hand, works only when aircraft power is on.'},
 {id:'fire-005',ch:'fire',lvl:2,q:'On the glareshield extinguisher switchlight, what do the amber \'D\' and green \'OK\' segments mean?',a:['D: nacelle access door open. OK: cowling closed and latched for flight','D: bottle due for inspection. OK: bottle within its date','D: bottle discharged (empty). OK: squib circuit good during test','D: detector loop active. OK: fire is out after discharge'],c:2,e:'The red segment shows a fire warning and is pushed to fire the bottle. The amber D lights when the bottle is empty. The green OK appears during the extinguisher test, confirming the firing circuit.'},
 {id:'fire-006',ch:'fire',lvl:1,q:'How many portable fire extinguishers are carried as standard, and where are they?',a:['One: under the pilot\'s seat, intended for both cockpit and cabin use','Three: in the cockpit, the cabin and the aft baggage area','Two: under the copilot\'s seat and in the cabin near the door','Two: both in the cockpit, one under each crew seat'],c:2,e:'One hand extinguisher is under the copilot\'s seat for the cockpit. The other is on the cabin floor near the entrance door.'},
 {id:'fire-007',ch:'fire',lvl:2,q:'Where are the engine fire bottle pressure gauges, and what affects their reading?',a:['On the overhead panel; the reading must be exactly 600 psi','On the glareshield next to each switchlight; no correction','Under the engine cowling, so they are readable only by maintenance staff','In the wheel wells; the correct reading depends on temperature'],c:3,e:'The gauges are on the bottles in the wheel wells and are checked at walkaround. Because the agent\'s pressure changes with temperature, the gauge must be compared with a pressure-versus-temperature chart.'},
 {id:'fire-008',ch:'fire',lvl:3,q:'In climb the red R ENG FIRE warning comes on. After you secure the right engine per the QRH, the light goes out before you discharge the bottle. How should you interpret this?',a:['The extinguisher has discharged by itself, so the bottle is now empty','The detector resets as it cools, so the fire may be out; it can still detect a re-ignition','The detector has failed and its indication should now be ignored for the rest of the flight','The warning was false and the engine may be restarted without restriction'],c:1,e:'If the wiring and sensor are intact, the warning goes out when the fire is out and the system can warn again. Treat it with caution, monitor, and land as soon as possible according to the QRH.'},
 {id:'fire-009',ch:'fire',lvl:2,q:'Which statement about the engine extinguishing system is correct?',a:['The bottles recharge in flight from the oxygen system','Both bottles can be fired into either engine by a selector','Each bottle serves only its own engine and fires once','Firing a bottle also closes that firewall valve automatically'],c:2,e:'Each side is independent and single-shot. Closing the firewall valve, feathering and cutting fuel are separate crew actions before the bottle is fired.'},
 {id:'fire-010',ch:'fire',lvl:2,q:'Where does the fire detection system get its power on a later B200?',a:['The selected inverter, through the 26 V AC bus','The hot battery bus, through the FIRE EXT fuses','Dual-fed bus No. 1, through the FIRE DET breaker','The right generator bus, through a 50 A feeder'],c:2,e:'Detection is powered from dual-fed bus No. 1. The extinguisher squibs are on the hot battery bus instead.'},
 {id:'pneu-001',ch:'pneu',lvl:1,q:'What pressure is the pneumatic bleed air regulated to, and what is the normal gauge range?',a:['About 4 psi; green arc 2–6 psi','About 18 psi; green arc 12–20 psi','About 6.5 psi; green arc 6.0–6.6 psi','About 30 psi; green arc 20–40 psi'],c:1,e:'The pneumatic regulator delivers about 18 psi. The gauge on the copilot\'s subpanel shows a 12–20 psi green arc.'},
 {id:'pneu-002',ch:'pneu',lvl:2,q:'Which systems use regulated pneumatic (bleed) air on the B200?',a:['Windshield heat, prop deice, rudder boost and the outflow valve','Engine starters, boots, flap motor and the stall-warning vane','Gear actuators, wheel brakes, door seal and the windshield heaters on both sides','Boots, rudder boost, door seal, leak-warning lines, hour meter, brake deice'],c:3,e:'Pneumatic air is taken off before the flow control units. Through an ejector it also creates the vacuum used by the instruments, the pressurisation controller and the boot hold-down.'},
 {id:'pneu-003',ch:'pneu',lvl:2,q:'How is vacuum produced on the B200, and what uses it?',a:['A bleed-air ejector; gyros, pressurisation controller, valves, boot hold-down','An electric vacuum pump in the nose; gyros and the pressurisation controller only','Cabin differential pressure; outflow valve and gyros only','An engine-driven vacuum pump; gyros and boot hold-down only'],c:0,e:'There is no vacuum pump: pneumatic air flowing through an ejector produces the suction. Losing pneumatic air therefore also means losing vacuum.'},
 {id:'pneu-004',ch:'pneu',lvl:1,q:'What is the normal suction range from sea level to 15,000 ft?',a:['1.0–2.0 in Hg','12–20 in Hg','3.0–4.3 in Hg','4.3–5.9 in Hg'],c:3,e:'The wide green arc (4.3–5.9 in Hg) applies up to 15,000 ft. Higher up, the narrow arc allows lower values (about 2.8–4.3 in Hg).'},
 {id:'pneu-005',ch:'pneu',lvl:3,q:'If one engine is shut down, are the pneumatic systems still available?',a:['Only the door seal, because it has its own small regulator','Yes; one engine is enough, and check valves stop loss through the dead one','Only if the dead engine\'s bleed-air valve is left at OPEN','No; deice boots and rudder boost both stop until the engine is restarted'],c:1,e:'The bleed lines join a common manifold with check valves. A single engine supplies all pneumatic needs, though brake deice is the biggest user and can limit rudder boost, as described elsewhere.'},
 {id:'pneu-006',ch:'pneu',lvl:2,q:'How does the bleed-air leak warning system detect a ruptured bleed line?',a:['Heat melts plastic tubing beside the duct; the pressure loss trips a switch','A thermocouple in each nacelle senses temperature above 300°F','A smoke detector under the cabin floor senses the hot air','A pressure switch in the pneumatic gauge line senses a drop below 12 psi and warns'],c:0,e:'The follower tubing works as a heat-sensitive fuse along the bleed ducts. Once it has melted, the warning stays on even after the leak is isolated.'},
 {id:'pneu-007',ch:'pneu',lvl:3,q:'In cruise the red L BL AIR FAIL warning comes on. What do you do, and how can you tell whether the leak has been isolated?',a:['No action unless the cabin altitude starts to rise; then select DUMP','Shut the left engine down at once, then select both bleed valves closed','INST & ENVIR OFF on the left; torque up and ITT down suggest isolation; light stays on','ENVIR OFF on the left side only; the red light should then go out within about a minute'],c:2,e:'INST & ENVIR OFF closes both the environmental and pneumatic valves on that side. Because the melted tubing cannot recover, the light stays on, so engine indications are your feedback. Rudder boost is also disarmed in that position.'},
 {id:'pneu-008',ch:'pneu',lvl:2,q:'What do the three BLEED AIR VALVE switch positions do?',a:['They select which engine pressurises the cabin: left, right or both','OPEN: both open. ENVIR OFF: cabin flow off, pneumatic on. INST & ENVIR OFF: both closed','OPEN: cabin flow only. ENVIR OFF: pneumatic only. INST & ENVIR OFF: warning test position','OPEN: pneumatic only. ENVIR OFF: both closed. INST & ENVIR OFF: both open'],c:1,e:'ENVIR OFF stops bleed air into the cabin (for example for maximum ground cooling) but keeps pneumatic services. INST & ENVIR OFF isolates the engine\'s bleed completely.'},
 {id:'pneu-009',ch:'pneu',lvl:2,q:'What does the flight hour meter need in order to run?',a:['Pneumatic pressure, DC power, and the right squat switch showing airborne','Pneumatic pressure only; it runs whenever an engine is turning','DC power only, with the battery switch ON and the avionics master ON','Engine oil pressure on either engine, and the left gear squat switch showing airborne'],c:0,e:'The meter runs only in flight, with pneumatic air and DC available and the right gear squat switch showing airborne.'},
 {id:'pneu-010',ch:'pneu',lvl:3,q:'On the ground at low N1, with brake deice on, you cycle the deice boots and both BL AIR FAIL lights flash briefly, then go out. What is the likely explanation?',a:['The boots have burst and are leaking regulated air into the wing leading edges','Both bleed-air ducts have ruptured; close both bleed valves now','A brief pneumatic pressure drop at low N1; normal if they go out at once','The leak-warning tubing has melted; the aircraft is no-go'],c:2,e:'Brake deice and boot inflation together can briefly pull regulated pressure down at low power. Momentary lights that go out immediately can be ignored; a steady light cannot.'},
 {id:'ice-001',ch:'ice',lvl:2,q:'Which of the following are anti-ice systems (preventing ice) rather than deice systems (removing ice)?',a:['Windshield, pitot, stall-vane and fuel-vent heat, and the inlet lips','Inertial separators, tail boots and the brake deice','Propeller boots, wing boots and the windshield wipers','Wing boots, tail boots, propeller boots and the engine inertial separators'],c:0,e:'The heated items keep ice from forming. Wing and tail boots, and the propeller boots, shed ice that has already formed, though the prop system should be managed like an anti-ice system.'},
 {id:'ice-002',ch:'ice',lvl:1,q:'What happens during one automatic deice boot cycle?',a:['Wings inflate about 6 s, then tail about 4 s, then all held down','Tail inflates about 6 s, then wings about 4 s, repeating until switched off','Left wing then right wing, about 30 s each, repeating','All boots inflate together for about 10 s, then held down'],c:0,e:'A timer runs the wing boots then the tail boots once per selection of SINGLE. Another cycle needs the switch selected again.'},
 {id:'ice-003',ch:'ice',lvl:2,q:'If electrical power to the deice system is lost, what happens to the boots?',a:['They inflate and stay inflated until after landing','They revert to a fixed automatic cycle','They can\'t inflate, but vacuum holds them flat','They deflate and flap loosely in the airflow'],c:2,e:'Inflation needs electrical control of the distributor valve. Without it, the ejector vacuum still holds the boots down, so the aerodynamic shape is preserved.'},
 {id:'ice-004',ch:'ice',lvl:1,q:'Below what outside air temperature should the deice boots not be operated?',a:['0°C','About −55°C','−20°C','−40°C'],c:3,e:'Operating the deice boots below −40°C OAT is prohibited by a published caution, so check OAT before cycling them in very cold air.'},
 {id:'ice-005',ch:'ice',lvl:2,q:'How does the MANUAL position of the deice switch work?',a:['It runs the automatic cycle continuously until OFF','It inflates the tail boots only, for as long as the switch is held','It latches all boots inflated until OFF is selected','All boots inflate while held, then deflate when released'],c:3,e:'MANUAL is the backup if the timer fails. Holding the boots inflated too long can let new ice form on the expanded shape.'},
 {id:'ice-006',ch:'ice',lvl:1,q:'What minimum speed should be held in sustained icing conditions, and why?',a:['140 KIAS, to avoid ice on the unprotected lower wing','160 KIAS, to keep the inertial separators fully effective','121 KIAS, to keep single-engine climb performance','180 KIAS, to keep the deice boots shedding properly'],c:0,e:'At lower speeds and higher angles of attack, ice collects behind the boots on the lower wing surface where it cannot be removed.'},
 {id:'ice-007',ch:'ice',lvl:1,q:'Up to what speed is windshield heat fully effective in sustained icing?',a:['259 KIAS','226 KIAS','200 KIAS','About 181 KIAS'],c:1,e:'At high speed and low temperature the windshield heaters may not keep up. If the windshield ices, slow to 226 KIAS or less.'},
 {id:'ice-008',ch:'ice',lvl:2,q:'What is the difference between NORMAL and HI windshield heat, and which side effect must you allow for?',a:['HI heats both panes, NORMAL only the pilot\'s; the radios are affected','NORMAL is for the ground, HI for flight; there is no side effect','HI heats a smaller key area harder; the standby compass is affected','HI raises temperature to about 150°F; the stall warning is affected'],c:2,e:'Each windshield has its own controller holding roughly 95–105°F. The heating currents disturb the standby compass and can also distort visual cues.'},
 {id:'ice-009',ch:'ice',lvl:1,q:'What is the normal propeller deice ammeter reading on a four-blade B200 in automatic mode?',a:['14–18 A','5–10 A','About 30–40 A','18–24 A'],c:3,e:'18–24 A is normal for four-blade installations (14–18 A for three-blade). The reading should be about the same as the timer steps through its sequence.'},
 {id:'ice-010',ch:'ice',lvl:2,q:'In automatic mode on later aircraft, how is propeller heat cycled?',a:['Both propellers heated together, continuously','About 90 s right propeller, then about 90 s left','About 30 s per blade, one blade at a time, in turn','Inner then outer segments, about 10 s each'],c:1,e:'The timer alternates between propellers, which keeps the electrical load down. Older aircraft with split boots used 30-second steps through outer and inner elements.'},
 {id:'ice-011',ch:'ice',lvl:3,q:'In icing conditions the prop deice ammeter suddenly reads zero in AUTO. What is a sensible troubleshooting path?',a:['Raise prop rpm to 2,200 rpm and keep AUTO selected until current returns','Ignore it; the ammeter often reads zero between timer steps in AUTO mode','Check AUTO switch; reset after 30 s; if no current, use MANUAL and watch loadmeters','Pull both prop deice breakers and leave icing at once; manual mode is not approved'],c:2,e:'Zero current points to a power or timer problem. The manual system heats the boots directly, and its current appears only as a rise on the generator loadmeters. Follow the QRH.'},
 {id:'ice-012',ch:'ice',lvl:3,q:'In icing the prop deice ammeter reads just below the green arc and a slight vibration develops. What does the guidance suggest?',a:['Switch prop deice off at once and leave icing','Keep AUTO on; if it vibrates, briefly raise prop rpm','Feather the vibrating propeller to stop the imbalance','Switch to MANUAL and hold it for ten minutes'],c:1,e:'Losing one heating element does not mean the whole system must be switched off. A brief rpm increase raises centrifugal force and helps shed uneven ice.'},
 {id:'ice-013',ch:'ice',lvl:2,q:'When must propeller deice not be operated?',a:['Whenever the inertial separators are extended in flight','During take-off','When the propellers are not turning (static)','Below 10,000 ft'],c:2,e:'Without rotation there is no cooling airflow or centrifugal shedding, and the boots can overheat and be damaged.'},
 {id:'ice-014',ch:'ice',lvl:1,q:'When must the engine inertial separators (ice vanes) be extended, and when should they be retracted?',a:['Extend at +5°C or colder before visible moisture; retract at +15°C and above','Extend at 0°C or colder in cloud only; retract above +5°C after leaving cloud','Extend only when ice is seen on the windshield; retract once it has cleared','Extend at +15°C or colder before visible moisture; retract at +5°C and above'],c:0,e:'Engine icing can occur without airframe ice, so extend early if in doubt. Retract in warm air to keep enough airflow through the oil cooler.'},
 {id:'ice-015',ch:'ice',lvl:3,q:'What cockpit change do you see when the ice vanes extend at normal cruise power?',a:['ITT drops a lot and torque rises slightly','Torque falls noticeably; ITT rises a little','No change at all at cruise power settings','N1 drops to idle until the vanes are retracted'],c:1,e:'Extended vanes disturb the inlet airflow. Torque drops noticeably, which also helps confirm the vanes have moved, and ITT rises a little. Torque can be restored with the power levers within ITT limits.'},
 {id:'ice-016',ch:'ice',lvl:3,q:'On a later B200 you select ENG ANTI-ICE ON and the amber ENG ICE FAIL caution comes on immediately. What is the likely cause and remedy?',a:['Likely main actuator power loss; select the STANDBY actuator','Normal: the amber light only shows that the vanes are moving out','Bleed-air overheat; close that engine\'s bleed-air valve','Inlet lip heat failure; slow to 160 KIAS or less'],c:0,e:'The caution means the vane has not reached the selected position. Timing helps diagnose the fault, and the standby actuator is the backup in either case. Watch torque to confirm the vane position.'},
 {id:'ice-017',ch:'ice',lvl:2,q:'How is the PT6 air inlet lip protected from ice on the B200?',a:['Hot oil routed from the oil cooler','A pneumatic boot cycled with the wing boots','Exhaust gas runs through it automatically','An electric blanket switched by the pilot'],c:2,e:'Some exhaust gas is ducted through the lip continuously. The pilot manages only the inertial separator to protect the compressor screen.'},
 {id:'ice-018',ch:'ice',lvl:2,q:'Why should pitot and stall-warning heat not be left on for long periods on the ground?',a:['They draw fuel from the vents and can starve the engines on the ground','Elements overheat without airflow; pitot heat has no thermal protection','They disable the yaw damper until the aircraft becomes airborne','They trigger the ALT WARN pressure switch while the aircraft is on the ground'],c:1,e:'Use them on the ground only briefly, for a check or to clear ice. Pitot heat should still be on for take-off when icing is suspected.'},
 {id:'ice-019',ch:'ice',lvl:2,q:'Which statements about optional brake deice are correct?',a:['Hydraulic fluid heated by the engines; limited to 5 minutes per flight, gear down only','Electric; no timer; usable at any temperature; no effect on ITT','Bleed air; 10-min timer after gear up; not continuous above +15°C; ITT +20°C','Bleed air; works only with the gear retracted; ITT drops by about 20°C'],c:2,e:'Brake deice is the biggest single user of bleed air. Its ITT penalty and temperature limits must be respected, and both instrument bleed-air valves must be open.'},
 {id:'ice-020',ch:'ice',lvl:3,q:'Descending in icing with brake deice on, you plan an idle descent. What must you keep in mind for the boots?',a:['Boots stop working below 10,000 ft, so ignore them','Brake deice raises bleed pressure, so nothing to consider','Use idle; the boots inflate best at low bleed pressure','Keep about 85 % N1 or more for proper boot inflation'],c:3,e:'With hot brakes taking a lot of bleed air, low N1 can leave too little pressure for proper boot inflation. Plan the descent power accordingly, or turn brake deice off.'},
 {id:'air-001',ch:'air',lvl:1,q:'What type of air-conditioning system does the B200 have, and where is the compressor?',a:['Vapour-cycle, compressor belt-driven on the right engine','Vapour-cycle, with an electric compressor in the tail cone','Air-cycle machine, driven by bleed air from both engines','Vapour-cycle, with compressors on both engines'],c:0,e:'Cooling comes from a refrigerant system. Its compressor is on the right engine accessory pad, and the condenser, receiver-dryer, expansion valve and evaporator are in the nose.'},
 {id:'air-002',ch:'air',lvl:3,q:'Why does the green AIR COND N1 LOW advisory sometimes appear at low idle on the ground?',a:['Refrigerant charge low, sensed by the sight-glass switch','Left engine not running, so the compressor is unpowered','Right engine below about 62 % N1 with cooling requested','Cabin temperature selector left in the AUTO position'],c:2,e:'An N1 speed switch keeps the compressor clutch disengaged below about 62 % N1 on the right engine. Raising that engine\'s N1 (for example towards high idle) lets the compressor engage.'},
 {id:'air-003',ch:'air',lvl:1,q:'What is the cooling capacity with the forward evaporator only, and with the optional aft evaporator?',a:['About 18,000 and 32,000 BTU','About 10,000 and 20,000 BTU','About 12,000 and 18,000 BTU','About 32,000 and 64,000 BTU'],c:0,e:'The aft evaporator adds cooling to the rear cabin, but only while the aft blower runs.'},
 {id:'air-004',ch:'air',lvl:2,q:'Why is air from the ceiling eyeball outlets always cooler than air from the floor outlets when the bleed air valves are open?',a:['The ceiling outlets have their own refrigerant coils behind each eyeball','Ram air feeds the ceiling outlets whenever the aircraft is pressurised','The floor outlets are heated electrically whenever either of the bleed valves is open','Bleed air is mixed into the floor duct; the ceiling gets only cooled recirculated air'],c:3,e:'The floor duct carries warm, pressurised bleed air mixed with recirculated air. The overhead outlets take only cooled recirculated air.'},
 {id:'air-005',ch:'air',lvl:2,q:'Why is no outside (ambient) air mixed with bleed air on the ground?',a:['The B200 has no ambient air valves; it uses bleed air only','Ambient air is only admitted above about 10,000 ft by an aneroid','The ambient valves close whenever air conditioning is on','The left gear safety switch holds the ambient valves closed'],c:3,e:'On the ground the ambient valves are held closed. After lift-off ambient air is blended in (left side first, then right a few seconds later) until it gets cold enough for the controller to close it off again.'},
 {id:'air-006',ch:'air',lvl:1,q:'Roughly what duct temperature turns on the amber DUCT OVERTEMP caution?',a:['About 204°F (96°C)','About 150°F','About 300°F','About 500°F'],c:2,e:'The caution means the heating duct air is very hot, usually from low airflow. 204°F is the figure for the bleed-air leak tubing, not for duct overtemperature.'},
 {id:'air-007',ch:'air',lvl:2,q:'Which statement about the optional electric cabin heat on later aircraft is correct?',a:['Replaces bleed-air heating entirely, including in flight','Ground only, both blowers on, about 300 A, cut off by the squat switch','Usable in flight to save bleed air, as long as the vent blower alone is running','Works from the battery alone, without a GPU or generator'],c:1,e:'Electric heat is a ground convenience, usually used with a GPU. Before switching the blowers off, check that the ELEC HEAT ON advisory has gone out.'},
 {id:'air-008',ch:'air',lvl:3,q:'On a hot ramp you want maximum cooling before taxi. Which bleed-air setting helps, and why?',a:['CABIN PRESS to DUMP, to open the fresh-air door','OPEN on both sides, to maximise the total airflow','ENVIR OFF, so no hot bleed air enters the cabin','INST & ENVIR OFF, so pneumatics are also off'],c:2,e:'ENVIR OFF stops warm bleed air entering the cabin, leaving the vapour-cycle system to cool recirculated air. Open the bleed valves again before take-off for pressurisation.'},
 {id:'air-009',ch:'air',lvl:2,q:'In manual temperature mode, how long should you hold the MANUAL TEMP switch to drive the bypass valves fully, and why does it matter for cooling?',a:['About 2 min per valve; both valves move together','About 5 s; the valves move quickly and the compressor is not affected by their position','About 1 min (30 s per valve); compressor runs only with bypass valves closed','About 10 min; the compressor runs only in AUTO mode anyway'],c:2,e:'The two bleed-air bypass valves move one after the other. To make sure the compressor can engage, select MAN COOL and hold DECR for about a minute.'},
 {id:'air-010',ch:'air',lvl:2,q:'When can fresh ram air be brought into the cabin through the fresh-air door?',a:['At any time with both bleed-air valves at ENVIR OFF','Whenever the vent blower is selected to HI, in any phase of flight','Only unpressurised, with CABIN PRESS at DUMP and zero differential','Only on the ground, with the gear safety switch compressed'],c:2,e:'The fresh-air door opens only with the cabin dumped and zero differential. Flying with the bleed valves out of OPEN also leaves you unpressurised, but without ram air.'},
 {id:'press-001',ch:'press',lvl:1,q:'What is the normal maximum cabin pressure differential of the B200?',a:['7.5 psi','6.0 ±0.1 psi','6.5 ±0.1 psi','5.5 psi'],c:2,e:'The B200 runs at 6.5 psid. Early 200s used 6.0, and later 200s 6.1.'},
 {id:'press-002',ch:'press',lvl:1,q:'At the maximum differential, what cabin altitude does a B200 have at FL350?',a:['About 10,400 ft','About 8,000 ft','About 11,700 ft cabin','About 12,500 ft'],c:0,e:'6.5 psid at 35,000 ft gives a cabin of about 10,400 ft, below the 12,500 ft ALT WARN threshold. The 6.0 psid King Air 200 would be at about 11,700 ft.'},
 {id:'press-003',ch:'press',lvl:2,q:'At FL310 the B200 cabin sits at about 8,600 ft, while an early King Air 200 at the same level shows about 9,900 ft. Why?',a:['The B200\'s larger outflow valve lets more air escape','The B200 controller uses a slower cabin rate setting','The 200 mixes ambient air into the pressurisation flow','The B200\'s higher maximum differential (6.5 vs 6.0 psi)'],c:3,e:'Cabin altitude at maximum differential depends only on aircraft altitude and differential. The extra 0.5 psi gives the B200 a noticeably lower cabin.'},
 {id:'press-004',ch:'press',lvl:1,q:'What ranges do the pressurisation controller\'s cabin-altitude and rate knobs cover?',a:['Cabin 0 to 10,000 ft; rate fixed at about 500 ft/min','Cabin 0 to 35,000 ft; rate about 500 to 5,000 ft/min','Cabin −1,000 to 12,500 ft; rate about 100 to 1,000 ft/min','Cabin −1,000 to 15,000 ft; rate about 200 to 2,000 ft/min'],c:3,e:'The outer scale of the dial shows selected cabin altitude, and the inner ACFT ALT scale shows the highest aircraft altitude that keeps that cabin at maximum differential.'},
 {id:'press-005',ch:'press',lvl:2,q:'How should the controller be set before take-off?',a:['ACFT ALT about 500–1,000 ft above cruise; comfortable rate','ACFT ALT at 35,000 ft, whatever the planned cruise','CABIN ALT at 10,000 ft; rate set to the maximum','CABIN ALT at field elevation; rate set to the minimum'],c:0,e:'Setting the aircraft-altitude scale slightly above cruise means the cabin reaches its selected altitude as you level off, without hitting maximum differential. If the cruise level changes by 1,000 ft or more, reset the dial.'},
 {id:'press-006',ch:'press',lvl:2,q:'What cabin altitude should be set for descent and landing?',a:['About 1,000 ft below field pressure altitude','Exactly the field pressure altitude','The same cabin altitude as in the cruise','About 500 ft above field pressure altitude'],c:3,e:'Setting slightly above the field means the cabin is depressurised before touchdown, with no pressure bump at landing. Adjust the rate for comfort and keep the aircraft descent rate from catching up with the cabin.'},
 {id:'press-007',ch:'press',lvl:2,q:'How does the outflow valve control cabin pressure?',a:['Aft bulkhead valve that opens only with DUMP','Bleed-air inlet valve that meters cabin inflow','Nose-mounted electric valve driven by the selected inverter','Aft bulkhead valve, vacuum-modulated by the controller'],c:3,e:'Inflow is roughly constant, from the flow control units. Cabin pressure is set by how much air the outflow valve lets out, under the controller\'s vacuum-operated command.'},
 {id:'press-008',ch:'press',lvl:2,q:'Which of these is NOT a function of the safety valve?',a:['Metering bleed air into the cabin','Depressurising the cabin when DUMP is selected','Keeping the aircraft unpressurised on the ground and preventing negative differential','Relieving pressure if the outflow valve fails to limit differential'],c:0,e:'The safety valve next to the outflow valve provides over-pressure relief, dump, ground depressurisation (left gear safety switch) and negative-pressure relief. Inflow is the job of the flow control units.'},
 {id:'press-009',ch:'press',lvl:2,q:'What do the DUMP and TEST positions of the CABIN PRESS switch do?',a:['DUMP drops the passenger masks; TEST checks the ALT WARN light and its pressure switch','DUMP and TEST both open the outflow valve for ground checks','DUMP opens the safety valve; TEST closes it and bypasses the squat switch','DUMP closes both bleed valves; TEST opens the outflow valve'],c:2,e:'TEST is spring-loaded and used for ground pressurisation checks. DUMP is lever-locked and stays selected until you move it back to PRESS.'},
 {id:'press-010',ch:'press',lvl:3,q:'With the controller set for a FL250 cruise, ATC clears you to FL330 and you forget to reset the dial. What happens as you climb above the ACFT ALT scale value?',a:['Differential hits the relief limit, so the cabin climbs with the aircraft','The system dumps cabin pressure automatically at FL250','The flow control units shut off the bleed air at FL250','The cabin stays at its setting and the fuselage is overstressed'],c:0,e:'Above the aircraft altitude the controller allows for, the outflow and safety valves take over to limit differential. The cabin therefore rises as you climb. Reset the dial for the new level.'},
 {id:'press-011',ch:'press',lvl:1,q:'At what cabin altitude does the red ALT WARN warning come on?',a:['About 12,500 ft','About 15,000 ft cabin','About 10,000 ft','About 14,000 ft'],c:0,e:'A pressure switch on the forward bulkhead turns on ALT WARN at about 12,500 ft cabin altitude, and the passenger masks deploy at about the same point if the system is armed.'},
 {id:'press-012',ch:'press',lvl:3,q:'At FL280 ALT WARN comes on and the cabin is climbing quickly. What are the first priorities?',a:['Troubleshoot the controller first, then don masks if the cabin keeps rising','Start a normal descent without masks, since the cabin is below 15,000 ft','Masks on at 100 %, crew communication, then descent decision and QRH','Select CABIN PRESS to TEST to close the safety valve, then continue'],c:2,e:'Time of useful consciousness at FL280 is only a few minutes, so oxygen comes first. With crew protected, descend as required and work through the QRH.'},
 {id:'press-013',ch:'press',lvl:2,q:'What prevents outside air pressure from exceeding cabin pressure during a rapid descent?',a:['The door seal deflating to let outside air in','The flow control units closing both bleed-air valves automatically','Negative-pressure relief in the outflow and safety valves','The ALT WARN switch opening the dump valve'],c:2,e:'Both valves can open the other way to stop the fuselage being loaded in reverse.'},
 {id:'press-014',ch:'press',lvl:2,q:'After lift-off, why does the left flow control unit\'s ambient air valve open a few seconds before the right?',a:['It is a built-in test of the leak warning','The right valve is only a standby unit','The left engine always provides more bleed air at lift-off','Staggered opening avoids a cabin pressure bump'],c:3,e:'Staggering the valves by about 6–8 seconds raises cabin inflow gradually, which is more comfortable for passengers.'},
 {id:'press-015',ch:'press',lvl:1,q:'What is the maximum operating pressure altitude of the B200?',a:['41,000 ft','About 31,000 ft','35,000 ft','28,000 ft'],c:2,e:'The B200 is certified to 35,000 ft. Early 200s were limited to 31,000 ft.'},
 {id:'press-016',ch:'press',lvl:3,q:'After take-off the cabin climbs at the same rate as the aircraft and the differential stays at zero. Which switch positions should you check first?',a:['AUTOFEATHER switch at ARM and both props at 2,000 rpm','CABIN PRESS at PRESS and both BLEED AIR VALVES at OPEN','YAW DAMP engaged and the rudder boost switch ON','INVERTER selector on No. 1 and AC volts in the green'],c:1,e:'With DUMP selected the safety valve stays open, and with the bleed valves out of OPEN there is no inflow. Either one leaves the cabin unpressurised, so these are the first things to check.'},
 {id:'press-017',ch:'press',lvl:1,q:'Where does the red arc start on the B200 cabin differential gauge?',a:['At 6.0 psi','At about 7.0 psi','At 6.1 psi','At 6.6 psi'],c:3,e:'For the B200, 0–6.6 psi is the approved (green) range and the red arc starts at 6.6 psi.'},
 {id:'hyd-001',ch:'hyd',lvl:1,q:'On B200s with hydraulic landing gear, what are the main parts of the gear power pack?',a:['28 V DC motor pump, bleed-pressurised reservoir, solenoid selector, pressure switch','Pneumatic actuators driven by bleed air, with a mechanical selector','A single hand pump with an electric selector and an accumulator','An engine-driven pump on each engine, an accumulator, and a manual gear selector valve'],c:0,e:'One electrically driven power pack supplies the three gear actuators. There is no engine-driven hydraulic pump on the aircraft.'},
 {id:'hyd-002',ch:'hyd',lvl:2,q:'With hydraulic gear, how is the gear held up after retraction?',a:['Mechanical uplock hooks on each gear leg and the doors','The gear doors alone, closed over the retracted wheels','Retract-side pressure, kept between about 2,475 and 2,775 psi','Friction in jackscrew actuators driven by an electric motor'],c:2,e:'Retraction takes about six seconds. The pressure switch then cycles the pump as needed to make up for slow leakage.'},
 {id:'hyd-003',ch:'hyd',lvl:3,q:'In cruise with the hydraulic gear retracted, the amber HYD FLUID LOW caution comes on. What is the concern and the sensible plan?',a:['Reservoir low, maybe a leak; prepare for the QRH hand-pump extension','Brake fluid is low in the master cylinders; avoid braking after landing','Engine oil is low on one engine; reduce power and monitor oil pressure','It is advisory only and needs no action until after landing'],c:0,e:'The caution comes from a level sensor in the power pack reservoir. Because the hand pump draws from a separate section, alternate extension remains the backup. Allow time and follow the QRH.'},
 {id:'hyd-004',ch:'hyd',lvl:2,q:'Why is the hydraulic gear selector valve spring-loaded to the DOWN position?',a:['So the gear cannot be lowered by the hand pump in flight','So that retraction is faster after take-off','So that pressure is kept on the brakes','So loss of electrical power fails towards gear-down'],c:3,e:'Fail-safe design: losing electrical power to the selector cannot hold the gear up by command. Combined with the hand pump this supports alternate extension.'},
 {id:'hyd-005',ch:'hyd',lvl:2,q:'The power pack reservoir has two sections. What is the advantage?',a:['One feeds the electric pump, one feeds the hand pump','One holds hot fluid and the other holds cold fluid','One section can be refilled in flight from the other','One feeds the gear, the other feeds the wheel brakes'],c:0,e:'Keeping a separate supply for the hand pump makes alternate extension more robust.'},
 {id:'hyd-006',ch:'hyd',lvl:3,q:'In cruise you notice the gear pump motor cycling on briefly from time to time. How should you interpret this?',a:['Normal top-up of retract pressure; frequent cycling suggests a leak','The gear is starting to extend because the selector has failed','Impossible; the pump is not powered once the gear is up','The selector valve has failed DOWN and the pump is fighting it'],c:0,e:'With no mechanical uplocks, pressure holds the gear up and the pump restores it when it falls to about 2,475 psi. Excessive cycling, especially with HYD FLUID LOW, needs attention.'},
 {id:'gear-001',ch:'gear',lvl:1,q:'What are the landing gear speed limits (VLO extend, VLO retract, VLE)?',a:['Extend 181 KIAS, retract 181 KIAS, extended 259 KIAS','Extend 200 KIAS, retract 181 KIAS, extended 200 KIAS','Extend 163 KIAS, retract 181 KIAS, extended 157 KIAS','Extend 181 KIAS, retract 163 KIAS, extended 181 KIAS'],c:3,e:'Retraction has the lowest limit (163 KIAS). Extension and flight with the gear down are both limited to 181 KIAS.'},
 {id:'gear-002',ch:'gear',lvl:2,q:'What stops the gear handle being raised on the ground, and how can it be overridden?',a:['A mechanical pin in the handle that the pilot removes during the before-take-off check','A solenoid hook, released in flight by the right squat switch; DN LOCK REL overrides it','A hydraulic lock in the selector valve, released by the left squat switch','The parking brake handle, which blocks the gear handle until it is released'],c:1,e:'The hook stops an accidental retraction on the ground. If the handle is forced UP on the ground, the red handle lights and warning horn activate (with DC power).'},
 {id:'gear-003',ch:'gear',lvl:1,q:'What indications do you see during and after normal gear extension?',a:['Green handle lights while in transit, then three red lights once all three gear are locked','Three amber lights in transit, then the handle light goes green','Red handle lights in transit, a green per gear as it locks, red out when all down','A single amber GEAR light in transit, then one green GEAR DOWN light'],c:2,e:'Three greens and no red in the handle means down and locked. A red handle light with a missing green means that gear is not locked.'},
 {id:'gear-004',ch:'gear',lvl:2,q:'On a B200 with flaps UP or APPROACH, when does the gear warning come on, and can it be silenced?',a:['Any power setting with gear not locked; horn and lights can both be silenced','Airspeed below 140 kt with gear not locked; neither horn nor lights can be silenced','Power lever below about 85 % N1 with gear not locked; horn and lights both silenceable','Power lever below about 85 % N1 with gear not locked; horn can be silenced, lights cannot'],c:3,e:'The power-lever trigger catches a gear-up approach with the flaps not fully down. Silencing stops only the horn, and advancing the lever re-arms the system.'},
 {id:'gear-005',ch:'gear',lvl:2,q:'With the flaps extended beyond APPROACH and the gear not down and locked, what happens?',a:['Horn and lights activate at any power; neither can be cancelled','Nothing happens unless both power levers are at idle','Horn sounds only at low power and can be silenced','Only an amber caution appears on the annunciator panel, with no horn'],c:0,e:'Selecting landing flaps with the gear up is a strong sign of a gear-up landing in progress, so the warning cannot be silenced.'},
 {id:'gear-006',ch:'gear',lvl:1,q:'On hydraulic-gear B200s, how is the gear lowered by the alternate method?',a:['Pull up the engage handle and ratchet the gear down by hand','Hold DN LOCK REL and cycle the handle until three greens show','Pull the LANDING GEAR RELAY CB, handle DN, hand-pump to three greens','Pull the gear handle out of its detent and let the gear free-fall'],c:2,e:'The hand pump is on the floor between the pilot\'s right foot and the pedestal. Electric-gear aircraft use a ratchet and engage handle instead.'},
 {id:'gear-007',ch:'gear',lvl:3,q:'During an alternate extension the three greens do not appear. What does the guidance say?',a:['Cycle the gear handle UP and DN, then pump again','Keep pumping until heavy resistance; extra pumping can\'t damage the gear','Reset the LANDING GEAR RELAY breaker and try a normal extension once more','Stop after about 50 strokes so the actuators are not damaged'],c:1,e:'A missing green can be a bulb or switch fault. Firm resistance is a better sign that the actuators are fully extended.'},
 {id:'gear-008',ch:'gear',lvl:2,q:'After a real alternate gear extension, why must you leave the controls, switches and circuit breakers alone until the aircraft is on jacks?',a:['The parking brake would release if the relay breaker is reset','The fault may be in the up circuit, so a reset could retract the gear','The battery would discharge through the gear relay overnight','The hand pump would lose its fluid back into the main reservoir section'],c:1,e:'The alternate system can only extend the gear. Disturbing the normal system after a real failure risks an unintended retraction, so it stays untouched until maintenance has found the fault.'},
 {id:'gear-009',ch:'gear',lvl:3,q:'On approach you select the gear down. After the normal time you have two greens (left and nose), the right green is missing and the handle light is red. What is a sensible order of actions?',a:['Land immediately, because a missing green light is almost always just a failed bulb filament','Cycle the gear up and down several times at a higher speed to free it','Raise the gear again and plan a gear-up landing on the long runway','Fly the aircraft below VLE, test the bulb, then QRH (alternate extension) with time to spare'],c:3,e:'First rule out a bulb fault, because the green lights can be pressed to test. A red handle light with a missing green means that gear may not be locked, so give yourself time and use the QRH.'},
 {id:'gear-010',ch:'gear',lvl:2,q:'How are the gear legs locked down on hydraulic-gear aircraft?',a:['All three: hydraulic pressure held on the extend side by the power pack pump','All three: over-centre drag braces only, with no internal locks','Mains: hook and plate on drag brace; nose: over-centre brace and actuator lock','Mains: friction in jackscrews; nose: the gear door linkage'],c:2,e:'Mechanical down-locks hold the gear once it is extended, so pressure is not needed to keep it down. That is also why the pump does not cycle after extension.'},
 {id:'gear-011',ch:'gear',lvl:1,q:'What is the nose-wheel steering range using the rudder pedals alone?',a:['About 45° left and 45° right of centre','About 14° left and 12° right','About 30° left and 30° right','About 12° left and 14° right'],c:1,e:'Direct pedal steering gives about 14° left and 12° right. Differential braking and differential power can increase this a lot for tight turns.'},
 {id:'gear-012',ch:'gear',lvl:3,q:'Why do the rudder pedals feel stiff and hardly steer when the aircraft is stationary?',a:['The rudder lock pin is still fitted','The steering is hydraulic and needs pump pressure to move','Steering is locked out below about 20 kt','The spring link compresses until the nose-wheel rolls'],c:3,e:'The spring link also lets the nose gear self-centre when it retracts, after which the pedals no longer act on it.'},
 {id:'gear-013',ch:'gear',lvl:2,q:'Which statement about the wheel brakes is correct?',a:['Only the pilot\'s pedals have brakes; the copilot\'s have none','The gear hydraulic pump powers the brakes through an accumulator and a valve','Either pilot\'s pedals work master cylinders; differential braking available','The brakes are pneumatic and use regulated bleed air'],c:2,e:'Each master cylinder feeds its own set of brakes, so braking can be differential. The parking brake holds the applied pressure in the lines.'},
 {id:'gear-014',ch:'gear',lvl:2,q:'Which group of functions is controlled by the LEFT main gear safety switch?',a:['Fuel crossfeed, the firewall valves, the standby boost pumps and the aux transfer','Safety valve, ambient valves, stall warning and its heat, hydraulic gear solenoid','Gear handle lock, flight hour meter, gear motor circuit and the parking brake','Autofeather arming, autoignition, the synchrophaser and the yaw damper'],c:1,e:'The left squat switch feeds several environmental and warning systems. The right one controls the gear handle lock, the gear motor circuits and the hour meter.'},
 {id:'gear-015',ch:'gear',lvl:2,q:'With high-flotation main gear, what difference do you see in flight?',a:['The main wheel wells have no doors at all','Part of each main wheel sticks out about five inches','The nose gear can be steered only by braking','The main gear retracts forwards instead of inwards into the nacelle'],c:1,e:'The bigger wheels and shorter struts do not fit fully in the wells, so the doors have cut-outs. This is a known configuration difference.'},
 {id:'gear-016',ch:'gear',lvl:3,q:'On a hydraulic-gear aircraft on the ground, the down-lock override is used by mistake and the handle is moved UP. What should happen?',a:['Handle lights and horn come on, and the selector circuit stays open','The gear retracts at once, because the override bypasses all protection','Only an amber caution appears and the gear starts to retract slowly','The parking brake releases and the nose gear retracts first'],c:0,e:'Several layers protect against retraction on the ground: the handle hook, the warning, and the selector circuit needing an airborne signal. Put the handle back DOWN at once.'},
 {id:'gear-017',ch:'gear',lvl:3,q:'After a practice alternate extension in the simulator or aircraft, how is the hydraulic gear retracted again?',a:['Pump the hand pump in the reverse direction until the gear is up','It cannot be retracted until the aircraft is on jacks','Reset the LANDING GEAR RELAY breaker and select the handle UP','Hold DN LOCK REL and pump while the handle is selected UP'],c:2,e:'Retraction is only possible through the normal powered system. After a real failure, though, nothing should be reset.'},
 {id:'flt-001',ch:'flt',lvl:1,q:'How are the B200\'s primary flight controls operated?',a:['Manually, by cables and push rods; flaps are electric','By pneumatic servos supplied with regulated bleed air','Fly-by-wire, with mechanical backup for the elevator','By hydraulic actuators powered by the gear power pack'],c:0,e:'The controls are conventional and unpowered. The only power assistance in the flight controls is the pneumatic rudder boost for asymmetric thrust.'},
 {id:'flt-002',ch:'flt',lvl:1,q:'Which surfaces have cockpit-adjustable trim tabs?',a:['Rudder, left elevator and the right aileron','Both elevators only; the rudder is untrimmed','Rudder, both elevators and the left aileron','Rudder, both elevators and both ailerons'],c:2,e:'Roll trim works through the left aileron tab. The right aileron\'s tab is set on the ground only.'},
 {id:'flt-003',ch:'flt',lvl:1,q:'What flap positions are selectable, and what are their deflections?',a:['APPROACH (30 %, about 10°) and DOWN (100 %, about 30°)','APPROACH (40 %, about 14°) and DOWN (100 %, about 35°)','APPROACH (50 %, about 20°) and DOWN (100 %, about 40°)','APPROACH (40 %, about 20°) and DOWN (100 %, about 45°)'],c:1,e:'The indicator reads in percent. APPROACH is 40 % (about 14°) and DOWN is 100 % (about 35°).'},
 {id:'flt-004',ch:'flt',lvl:2,q:'On a later B200, can the flaps be stopped at an intermediate position such as 60 %?',a:['Yes; pull the FLAP MOTOR breaker at the wanted point','No; only UP, APPROACH and DOWN can be selected','Yes; select DOWN, then APPROACH at the wanted point','Yes; hold the handle between detents until it stops'],c:1,e:'Later aircraft have follow-up flaps with three positions. Earlier aircraft allowed intermediate settings by returning the handle to the APPROACH detent.'},
 {id:'flt-005',ch:'flt',lvl:2,q:'What does the split-flap protection do?',a:['A 3–6° split on one side stops the motor until the fault is fixed','A 3–6° split sounds the stall horn until the flaps match','A 3–6° split retracts all four flaps automatically to the UP position','A 10° split between wings is corrected by the autopilot'],c:0,e:'Protection works between the two flaps on each side. A stopped flap must be handled with the QRH, and the flaps stay where they are.'},
 {id:'flt-006',ch:'flt',lvl:1,q:'What powers the flap motor?',a:['Dual-fed bus No. 1, through a 50 A FLAP MOTOR limiter','Dual-fed bus No. 3, through a 20 A FLAP MOTOR breaker','The hot battery bus, through a 20 A FLAP MOTOR fuse','The right generator bus, through the gear motor relay'],c:1,e:'The single electric motor drives flexible shafts to screwjacks at each flap.'},
 {id:'flt-007',ch:'flt',lvl:2,q:'How does the rudder boost system know when to help?',a:['It works whenever the yaw damper is engaged, at any power setting','A yaw-rate gyro senses yaw above about 5° per second and applies rudder','A torque difference of about 600 ft-lb between the engines triggers it','A switch senses a bleed-pressure difference of about 60 psi between engines'],c:3,e:'Rudder boost is armed with its switch ON and both bleed-air switches in OPEN or ENVIR OFF. Selecting INST & ENVIR OFF on either side disables it.'},
 {id:'flt-008',ch:'flt',lvl:3,q:'During the run-up you hold the left engine at idle and advance the right engine. Which rudder pedal should move forward if rudder boost is working?',a:['The right pedal, as rudder is applied against the yaw from the right engine','The left pedal, because the boost always pushes towards the idle engine','Both pedals move together, because the servos act symmetrically','Neither pedal, because rudder boost only works when airborne'],c:0,e:'The rudder boost differential-pressure switch senses the bleed-pressure difference and applies rudder against the yaw. With the right engine at high power the aircraft yaws left, so right rudder is applied and the right pedal moves forward. Repeat the test for the other side.'},
 {id:'flt-009',ch:'flt',lvl:1,q:'Above what altitude is an operational yaw damper required?',a:['About 25,000 ft','31,000 ft','17,000 ft','10,000 ft'],c:2,e:'Without a yaw damper, flight is limited to 17,000 ft. The yaw damper should be off for take-off and landing.'},
 {id:'flt-010',ch:'flt',lvl:2,q:'How is the optional electric elevator trim protected against runaway?',a:['Split switch needs both halves; two-level disconnect; manual trim always works','It runs only with the yaw damper on, which monitors pitch rate','Only the autopilot can drive it; the pilot uses the manual wheel','Each input is limited to 5 seconds; then the motor locks out until reset'],c:0,e:'Splitting the switch means one failed contact cannot drive the trim. Pressing the disconnect to its second level cuts power, and the system is reset by cycling its pedestal switch.'},
 {id:'flt-011',ch:'flt',lvl:1,q:'How far above the stall does the stall warning horn sound with the flaps up and with the flaps fully down?',a:['Flaps up about 8–14 kt; full flaps about 5–13 kt','Flaps up about 15–20 kt; full flaps about 10–15 kt','Flaps up about 5–13 kt; full flaps about 8–14 kt','Flaps up about 2–5 kt; full flaps about 2–5 kt'],c:2,e:'The lift computer adds flap position to the vane signal. With the flaps at APPROACH the margin is about 5–12 kt.'},
 {id:'flt-012',ch:'flt',lvl:2,q:'Where does the stall warning system sense angle of attack, and what disables it on the ground?',a:['Right wing vane; disabled on the ground by the right squat switch','Left wing vane; disabled on the ground by the left squat switch','Tail vane; disabled on the ground by the parking brake switch','Nose probe; disabled on the ground by the nose gear switch'],c:1,e:'The vane feeds a lift computer that sounds the horn. The STALL WARN TEST switch lifts the vane electrically for a pre-flight check.'},
 {id:'flt-013',ch:'flt',lvl:3,q:'After flying through icing you still have some ice on the wings. How far can you trust the stall warning?',a:['It is irrelevant, because ice does not change the stall','Even more than usual, because the ice makes it trigger earlier','Completely, because the vane and its plate are heated','Not fully; ice raises stall speed and may delay the warning'],c:3,e:'Vane heat protects only the vane itself, not the airflow over an iced wing. Fly with extra margin and expect higher stall speeds and longer landing distances.'},
 {id:'flt-014',ch:'flt',lvl:3,q:'Both engines are running, but the right engine\'s bleed-air valve switch is moved to INST & ENVIR OFF. A large power difference then develops during a go-around. What will rudder boost do?',a:['It boosts to the left only, using the left engine\'s bleed air','Nothing; INST & ENVIR OFF on either side disarms rudder boost','It applies full rudder both ways and the pedals lock','It boosts normally, because the left engine still supplies air'],c:1,e:'Rudder boost needs both bleed switches at OPEN or ENVIR OFF. Knowing this matters after closing a bleed valve, for example following a BL AIR FAIL.'},
 {id:'flt-015',ch:'flt',lvl:3,q:'Why could rudder boost be ineffective after an engine failure if brake deice is running?',a:['Brake deice switches off the yaw damper, which also arms the rudder boost','Brake deice uses so much bleed air that the sensed difference may be too small','Rudder boost is powered through the brake deice circuit breaker','Brake deice automatically closes both bleed-air valves in flight'],c:1,e:'Brake deice is the largest single user of bleed air. With one engine out and brake deice on, the boost system may not see enough differential to engage.'},
 {id:'avio-001',ch:'avio',lvl:1,q:'Which pitot source feeds which airspeed indicator?',a:['One belly pitot to both indicators, with a crossover','Both masts to both indicators through a common line','Left mast to the pilot, right mast to the copilot','Right mast to the pilot, left mast to the copilot'],c:2,e:'The two heated pitot tubes on the lower nose are independent. A blocked or unheated tube affects only that side.'},
 {id:'avio-002',ch:'avio',lvl:1,q:'Which statement about the normal static ports is correct?',a:['Separate pilot and copilot systems, ports on both sides, not heated','Separate systems, with heated ports on the pitot masts','Separate systems, with unheated ports inside the unpressurised nose bay','One shared system with a single heated port on each side'],c:0,e:'Having a port on each side cancels sideslip errors. Because they are unheated they can ice over, which is why there is an alternate source.'},
 {id:'avio-003',ch:'avio',lvl:2,q:'Where does the pilot\'s alternate static source take its air from, and what does it affect?',a:['Pressurised cabin; both pilots; only the VSI is affected','Copilot\'s static line; pilot only; only the altimeter is affected','Heated port on the left side; feeds both pilots; no correction needed at all','Unpressurised aft fuselage; pilot only; airspeed, altimeter and VSI affected'],c:3,e:'Use the AFM alternate-static correction charts for airspeed and altitude. The VSI is also affected, but there is no correction table for it.'},
 {id:'avio-004',ch:'avio',lvl:3,q:'In icing at night the pilot\'s altimeter freezes and the airspeed drifts during a climb, while the copilot\'s instruments behave normally. What is the most likely cause and the fix?',a:['Air data computer overheating: pull its breaker and reset','Pilot\'s static ports iced: select ALTERNATE, correct and cross-check','Pilot\'s pitot heat has failed: select the alternate static source and recheck','Inverter failed: select the other inverter and recheck'],c:1,e:'A frozen altimeter and airspeed changing with altitude are typical of a blocked static source. Since the copilot\'s side is fine, alternate static for the pilot is the logical step.'},
 {id:'avio-005',ch:'avio',lvl:1,q:'How are the two pitot heaters powered?',a:['Both from the hot battery bus, through fuses','Left from dual-fed bus 1, right from dual-fed bus 2','Both from the selected inverter, through the 115 V bus','Left from dual-fed bus 3, right from dual-fed bus 4'],c:1,e:'Putting them on separate buses means a single bus failure cannot take away pitot heat on both sides.'},
 {id:'avio-006',ch:'avio',lvl:1,q:'What are the outside air temperature limits for the B200?',a:['Min −54°C; max ISA +37°C at all altitudes','Min −60°C; max ISA +40°C to 25,000 ft, ISA +35°C above','Min −40°C; max ISA +31°C to 25,000 ft, ISA +20°C above','Min −60°C; max ISA +37°C to 25,000 ft, ISA +31°C above'],c:3,e:'The B200 minimum is −60°C (the 200 is −53.9°C). Above 25,000 ft the hot limit is lower.'},
 {id:'avio-007',ch:'avio',lvl:3,q:'The pilot and copilot altimeters disagree, and ATC reports a Mode C altitude that matches the copilot\'s. Which control decides which altimeter feeds the transponder?',a:['The alternate static source valve handle','The avionics master switch','The encoding-altimeter select switch','The transponder IDENT button'],c:2,e:'Typical installations let you pick the encoding source. Knowing which altimeter ATC sees helps you find out which one is wrong.'},
 {id:'avio-008',ch:'avio',lvl:2,q:'Which statement about the aural warning system is correct?',a:['It works only with the avionics master ON and the audio panel selected','It can be silenced for all warnings at once with a single switch','It sounds stall, gear, autopilot and altitude alerts, with no volume control','It sounds stall and gear alerts, at a volume set by the speaker knob'],c:2,e:'The tones go to headsets and speaker at a fixed volume so they cannot be turned down by accident.'},
 {id:'avio-009',ch:'avio',lvl:2,q:'When is the yaw damper used, and when should it be disengaged?',a:['Any altitude, required above 17,000 ft; off for take-off and landing','Only with one engine inoperative; engaged for the landing','Only above 17,000 ft; it must be engaged for both take-off and landing','Only on the ground for taxi; off for the whole of the flight'],c:0,e:'The yaw damper improves directional stability, especially at altitude. It is off for take-off and landing so it does not fight the pilot\'s rudder inputs.'},
 {id:'avio-010',ch:'avio',lvl:2,q:'Under commercial (Part 135-type) rules referenced in the supplement, what is the minimum en-route autopilot altitude above terrain?',a:['1,000 ft above terrain','50 ft above terrain','200 ft above terrain','500 ft above terrain'],c:3,e:'The autopilot supplement adds operating limits, including 500 ft above terrain en route and DH/MDA on coupled approaches. Check your actual autopilot supplement and operator rules.'},
 {id:'misc-001',ch:'misc',lvl:2,q:'Why is the standby magnetic compass a poor heading reference at some times?',a:['It is distorted by cabin pressure once the cabin is pressurised','It reads correctly only above FL200, where the field is steadier','It becomes erratic with windshield heat or air conditioning on','It is accurate only with the autopilot engaged and the gyros slaved'],c:2,e:'The current in the windshield heaters and air-conditioning components disturbs the compass, as a cockpit placard warns. Allow for this if you ever need the compass.'},
 {id:'misc-002',ch:'misc',lvl:1,q:'What rule applies to the windshield wipers?',a:['Never on a dry windshield; avoid them on a cracked outer pane','They can be left running on dry glass in PARK','Only with windshield heat on HI, to avoid smearing ice','Only above 160 KIAS, so the airflow can help clear the blades'],c:0,e:'Wiping a dry windshield scratches it, and a cracked outer panel can damage the wipers. PARK moves them to the inboard position.'},
 {id:'misc-003',ch:'misc',lvl:2,q:'What are the static wicks on the trailing edges and wingtips for?',a:['To act as small vortex generators that delay the stall at the tips','To act as lightning conductors for the fuel vents','To discharge precipitation static that causes radio noise','To balance the control surfaces against flutter'],c:2,e:'Precipitation static builds up in flight. The wicks bleed it away, and missing or broken wicks should be checked against the allowable limits.'},
 {id:'misc-004',ch:'misc',lvl:2,q:'What must be true for oxygen to flow from the first-aid outlet in the toilet area?',a:['CABIN PRESS switch at DUMP so the supply line can be charged','Cabin altitude above 12,500 ft so the passenger valve has opened','Crew SYS READY handle armed, then the outlet opened and its valve on','The passenger masks must already have deployed from the ceiling'],c:2,e:'The first-aid outlet is on the primary supply line, so it needs the crew system on. It does not depend on the passenger masks.'},
 {id:'misc-005',ch:'misc',lvl:1,q:'How long is it recommended to wait after refuelling before taking fuel samples, and why?',a:['About 3 hours, so water and dirt can settle','About 24 hours, so the additive can mix in','No wait; the capacitance probes detect water','About 5 minutes, so the fuel can cool down'],c:0,e:'Settling time makes sumping more effective. There are several sump drains and a firewall-filter drain on each wing to check.'},
 {id:'misc-006',ch:'misc',lvl:2,q:'What is the main risk of towing the aircraft with the rudder lock pin installed?',a:['Damage to the nose-wheel steering linkage','Damage to the elevator control cables','Damage to the left main gear torque knee and strut','Damage to the rudder trim tab actuator'],c:0,e:'The nose-wheel steering is linked to the rudder pedals. Remove the control locks before towing.'},
 {id:'wb-001',ch:'wb',lvl:1,q:'What is the maximum take-off weight of the B200?',a:['11,000 lb','12,500 lb','12,800 lb','14,000 lb'],c:1,e:'The take-off condition must not exceed 12,500 lb. Flight load-factor limits are also quoted at this weight.'},
 {id:'wb-002',ch:'wb',lvl:1,q:'What is the maximum zero-fuel weight of the B200?',a:['12,500 lb','11,000 lb','10,400 lb','9,800 lb'],c:1,e:'The B200\'s maximum zero-fuel weight is 11,000 lb (10,400 lb for the King Air 200).'},
 {id:'wb-003',ch:'wb',lvl:2,q:'Why is there a maximum zero-fuel weight at all?',a:['It protects the landing gear from overload while taxiing','It is only a CG limit, to keep the aircraft within its loading envelope','Fuel in the wings relieves wing-root bending; non-fuel weight does not','It limits pressurisation loads on the cabin structure'],c:2,e:'Fuel in the wings reduces wing-root bending in flight. Weight above that limit has to be fuel, so payload is capped regardless of take-off weight.'},
 {id:'wb-004',ch:'wb',lvl:2,q:'What does \'basic empty weight\' include?',a:['Empty aircraft plus crew, full oil and unusable fuel','Empty aircraft with no oil, no fuel and no fluids','Empty aircraft plus usable fuel but no engine oil','Empty aircraft plus unusable fuel and full engine oil'],c:3,e:'Basic empty weight is the starting point for loading: empty weight plus unusable fuel and the oil needed to fill the engines.'},
 {id:'wb-005',ch:'wb',lvl:2,q:'How do ramp weight and take-off weight relate?',a:['Take-off weight is ramp weight minus start, taxi and run-up fuel','Take-off weight is ramp weight plus the payload loaded at the ramp','They are always the same, because taxi fuel is ignored','Ramp weight is take-off weight minus the crew and baggage'],c:0,e:'The loading form builds up zero-fuel, then ramp, then take-off and landing conditions. Each must be checked against its weight limit and the CG envelope.'},
 {id:'wb-006',ch:'wb',lvl:2,q:'How is the CG position found from the loading form?',a:['Total moment divided by total weight, in inches aft of datum','Sum of all the arms, divided by the number of items','Total moment divided by the zero-fuel weight only','Total weight divided by total moment, in inches aft of datum'],c:0,e:'CG equals moment divided by weight. The form uses moment/100 to keep the numbers manageable, and the graph shows whether the point is within the envelope.'},
 {id:'wb-007',ch:'wb',lvl:3,q:'A charter load gives a zero-fuel weight of 11,250 lb. With the fuel needed you would be at 12,400 lb for take-off. Is the load acceptable?',a:['Yes: provided the CG is in the middle of the envelope','Yes: take-off weight is below 12,500 lb, so it is fine','No: ZFW exceeds 11,000 lb, so remove 250 lb of payload','No: take off with 250 lb less fuel to fix the ZFW'],c:2,e:'Every limit must be met on its own. Removing fuel does not help a zero-fuel-weight exceedance; only removing payload does.'},
 {id:'wb-008',ch:'wb',lvl:3,q:'An aircraft weighs 12,000 lb with a total moment of 2,160,000 lb-in (moment/100 = 21,600). Where is the CG?',a:['180.0 in aft of datum','120.0 in aft of datum','55.6 in aft of datum','216.0 in aft of datum'],c:0,e:'CG = 2,160,000 ÷ 12,000 = 180.0 in. Then check this point against the AFM envelope for 12,000 lb.'},
 {id:'wb-009',ch:'wb',lvl:2,q:'What standard fuel density is used for the B200\'s published fuel quantities, and what happens to fuel weight on a very cold day?',a:['7.5 lb/gal; density does not change with temperature','6.7 lb/gal; cold fuel is denser, so it weighs more','6.7 lb/gal; cold fuel is less dense, so it weighs less','6.0 lb/gal; cold fuel is less dense, so it weighs less'],c:1,e:'Published totals use 6.7 lb/gal. The loading charts include density variation, so use the actual density when filling to volume. The gauges themselves compensate for density.'},
 {id:'wb-010',ch:'wb',lvl:2,q:'After working out the take-off condition, why is the landing condition checked separately?',a:['Fuel burn changes weight and moment, so the CG can move','Landing weight is always greater than take-off weight','Only the landing weight is limited, never the CG','It is not needed if the take-off CG is within limits'],c:0,e:'Subtract the fuel used (weight and moment) from the take-off condition and check the result against the moment limits.'},
 {id:'perf-001',ch:'perf',lvl:1,q:'What is the published VMCA of the B200, and how is it marked?',a:['99 KIAS, bottom of the green arc','86 KIAS, red radial line','91 KIAS, blue radial line','121 KIAS, red radial line'],c:1,e:'VMCA is 86 KIAS (91 KCAS). The blue line at 121 KIAS is VYSE.'},
 {id:'perf-002',ch:'perf',lvl:1,q:'What is VYSE (blue line) for the B200?',a:['110 KIAS','100 KIAS','135 KIAS','121 KIAS'],c:3,e:'The blue radial marks the best rate-of-climb speed with one engine inoperative (122 KCAS / 121 KIAS). Use it in the second segment and for single-engine climbs.'},
 {id:'perf-003',ch:'perf',lvl:2,q:'Under which conditions is VMCA determined for certification?',a:['Any configuration at maximum landing weight with the yaw damper engaged','Critical engine feathered in advance, 15° bank, full flaps and a mid CG','Both engines at idle, wings level, gear down, full flaps and the most forward CG','Sudden failure, T/O power, ≤5° bank to live engine, gear up, T/O flaps, aft CG'],c:3,e:'Those conditions are deliberately demanding. In other conditions, for example with no bank or a forward CG, the real minimum control speed can be different, so do not rely on the red line alone.'},
 {id:'perf-004',ch:'perf',lvl:2,q:'What is VSSE?',a:['The speed for best climb at the single-engine service ceiling','The maximum structural cruising speed with one engine out','The stall speed in the take-off configuration with one engine out','The minimum speed for intentionally failing an engine in training'],c:3,e:'Engine cuts for training should not be made below VSSE, because a sudden failure close to VMCA leaves little margin. Use the value in your AFM.'},
 {id:'perf-005',ch:'perf',lvl:2,q:'What does VXSE give you?',a:['Best one-engine climb gradient (height per distance)','Best one-engine climb rate (height gained per minute)','The minimum control speed with one engine out','The best glide speed with both engines failed'],c:0,e:'Best angle (VXSE) maximises climb gradient and best rate (VYSE) maximises climb rate. Use VXSE only as long as obstacles require.'},
 {id:'perf-006',ch:'perf',lvl:2,q:'How is the accelerate-stop distance defined in the B200 performance data?',a:['Accelerate to V2, then stop with brakes and reverse on both engines','Accelerate to V1, lose an engine, continue and reach 35 ft at V2','Accelerate to V1, then stop with brakes and reverse on the live engine','Accelerate to VR, then stop with brakes only and no reverse at all'],c:2,e:'Accelerate-stop includes reverse on the live engine. Accelerate-go is the distance to continue after a failure at V1 and reach V2 at 35 ft.'},
 {id:'perf-007',ch:'perf',lvl:1,q:'In the B200 take-off data used here, how does V1 relate to VR?',a:['V1 is above V2','V1 is VMCA','V1 is always 10 kt below VR','V1 equals VR'],c:3,e:'In this performance data the take-off decision speed equals the rotation speed. So after an engine failure at or above V1 you rotate and continue.'},
 {id:'perf-008',ch:'perf',lvl:2,q:'What is a \'net\' take-off climb gradient?',a:['The actual gradient increased by 0.8 %','The gradient measured at sea level only','The actual gradient reduced by 0.8 %','The gradient with both engines operating'],c:2,e:'Net gradients are deliberately conservative. They are averaged from 35 ft to 1,500 ft above the runway with take-off flaps and the gear up.'},
 {id:'perf-009',ch:'perf',lvl:3,q:'At a high-altitude airport on a hot day, a training engine cut is planned. Why might the stall come before loss of directional control?',a:['The propellers turn faster in thin air and add yaw','Less power lowers VMCA, so the stall speed can become the limit','The yaw damper becomes less effective at high altitude','VMCA rises with density altitude and so overtakes the stall speed'],c:1,e:'VMCA falls as available power drops with altitude and temperature. In some weight and altitude combinations the stall then comes first, which is why training cuts use VSSE and good judgement.'},
 {id:'perf-010',ch:'perf',lvl:3,q:'Which change would make the actual minimum control speed HIGHER than in a normal situation?',a:['A more forward CG','A 5° bank towards the live engine','A more aft CG','Less power on the live engine'],c:2,e:'An aft CG reduces the rudder\'s leverage, so more speed is needed for the same yawing moment. A forward CG, less power, or a small bank towards the live engine all help.'},
 {id:'perf-011',ch:'perf',lvl:2,q:'With heavy ice on the airframe, how much longer can the landing distance become?',a:['Up to about twice the normal distance','About 10 % longer','About 25 % longer','No change, because touchdown speed is the same'],c:0,e:'Ice raises the stall speed, so approach speed must be higher, and touchdown energy rises. Landing distance may double.'},
 {id:'perf-012',ch:'perf',lvl:1,q:'What are the published stall speeds at maximum weight with idle power (bottom of the airspeed arcs) for the B200?',a:['About 60 KIAS full flaps; about 80 KIAS flaps up','About 86 KIAS full flaps; about 121 KIAS flaps up','About 99 KIAS full flaps; about 157 KIAS flaps up','About 75 KIAS full flaps; about 99 KIAS flaps up'],c:3,e:'The wide white arc starts at VSO (about 75 KIAS, full flaps) and the narrow white arc at VS (about 99 KIAS, flaps up). Real stall speeds rise with weight, bank and ice.'},
 {id:'perf-013',ch:'perf',lvl:2,q:'What does the \'demonstrated crosswind\' value represent?',a:['The maximum tailwind component allowed for take-off and landing','A hard structural limit for the landing gear on take-off and landing','The largest crosswind shown controllable in certification, not a hard limit','The crosswind above which coupled approaches are prohibited'],c:2,e:'Exceeding the demonstrated value is not automatically forbidden, but it has not been tested. Many operators set their own limit.'},
 {id:'perf-014',ch:'perf',lvl:3,q:'During a rejected take-off at high speed, how should reverse and brakes be used?',a:['Brakes work best at high speed, reverse only below 40 kt','Reverse works best at high speed, brakes at low speed','Maximum braking first; reverse is delayed to avoid yaw','Reverse is not allowed during a rejected take-off'],c:1,e:'Propeller drag is greatest at high speed, and brakes become more effective as speed falls. After an engine failure, reverse only the operating engine with care and keep straight.'},
 {id:'perf-015',ch:'perf',lvl:2,q:'Why is it recommended to come out of reverse at about 40 kt when possible?',a:['Because reverse stops working below 40 kt','To keep the brakes from overheating at low speed','To stop the nose-wheel lifting off under strong reverse thrust','To limit blade erosion and avoid blowing up dust or snow'],c:3,e:'At low speed the reversed propellers pick up gravel, dust or snow. Take extra care on loose or contaminated surfaces.'},
 {id:'perf-016',ch:'perf',lvl:3,q:'After an engine failure and feather, which configuration gives the best single-engine climb performance?',a:['Gear and flaps up, feathered, wings level with ball centred, VMCA','Gear and flaps up, feathered, about 5° bank to the live engine, VYSE','Gear down for stability, approach flaps, wings level, VYSE','Gear up, approach flaps, failed prop windmilling, 5° bank, VYSE'],c:1,e:'Drag reduction is everything with one engine. A small bank towards the live engine reduces sideslip, so the ball sits slightly towards the live engine.'},
 {id:'perf-017',ch:'perf',lvl:3,q:'On a one-engine-inoperative visual approach, which speed guideline applies until a landing is assured?',a:['Not below about VYSE until landing is assured, then flaps and VREF','VREF from the final approach fix, with full flaps selected early','181 KIAS until the threshold, then reduce to VREF','VMCA plus 5 kt from the final approach fix to the threshold'],c:0,e:'Holding blue-line speed or more keeps go-around and control margins. Full flaps and VREF come only when the landing is certain.'},
 {id:'perf-018',ch:'perf',lvl:3,q:'Why is a single-engine go-around late in the approach a serious decision on any light twin, including the B200?',a:['Autofeather may feather the good engine during the go-around','Reverse is armed late in the approach and may deploy','Low speed and full drag leave little climb and control margin','It isn\'t serious; the B200 climbs well in any configuration'],c:2,e:'Cleaning up the drag (gear, flaps), keeping speed above VYSE and making the decision early are what make a single-engine go-around safe.'},
 {id:'lim-001',ch:'lim',lvl:1,q:'What is VMO for the B200?',a:['269 KIAS','245 KIAS','279 KIAS','259 KIAS'],c:3,e:'VMO is 259 KIAS on the B200 (the earliest 200s had 269 KIAS). Never exceed it in any phase of flight.'},
 {id:'lim-002',ch:'lim',lvl:1,q:'What is the design manoeuvring speed (VA) at 12,500 lb?',a:['163 KIAS','About 157 KIAS','181 KIAS','200 KIAS'],c:2,e:'Above 181 KIAS, avoid full or abrupt control deflections. VA falls as weight decreases.'},
 {id:'lim-003',ch:'lim',lvl:1,q:'What are the flap speed limits (VFE) for the B200?',a:['APPROACH 200 KIAS; DOWN 157 KIAS','APPROACH 181 KIAS; DOWN 146 KIAS','APPROACH 157 KIAS; DOWN 121 KIAS','APPROACH 220 KIAS; DOWN 181 KIAS (B200)'],c:0,e:'The white triangle marks 200 KIAS for approach flaps, and the top of the white arc is 157 KIAS for full flaps (the King Air 200 was 146 KIAS).'},
 {id:'lim-004',ch:'lim',lvl:1,q:'What are the flight load-factor limits at 12,500 lb with the flaps up?',a:['+4.4 g and −1.76 g','+3.17 g and −1.27 g','+3.8 g and −1.52 g','+2.5 g and −1.0 g'],c:1,e:'With flaps up the B200 is limited to +3.17 / −1.27 g at maximum weight.'},
 {id:'lim-005',ch:'lim',lvl:1,q:'What are the B200 load-factor limits with the flaps down?',a:['+2.0 g and −1.27 g','+3.17 g and −1.27 g','+2.0 g and 0 g','+1.5 g and −0.5 g'],c:2,e:'With flaps extended the B200 allows +2.0 g and no negative g (the 200 allowed −1.27 g).'},
 {id:'lim-006',ch:'lim',lvl:1,q:'What is the maximum allowed ITT during a transient (for example an acceleration)?',a:['850°C','1,000°C','800°C','770°C'],c:0,e:'The transient ITT limit is 850°C. 800°C is the steady take-off and max continuous limit, and 1,000°C applies only to starting, for 5 s.'},
 {id:'lim-007',ch:'lim',lvl:1,q:'How long may take-off power be used, and what is maximum continuous power for?',a:['T/O power 1 min; max continuous 5 min for single-engine','T/O power 10 min; max continuous 30 min for climb','T/O power 5 min; max continuous not time-limited, for emergencies','T/O power is not time-limited; max continuous is limited to 5 minutes'],c:2,e:'The power terminology sets take-off power as the 5-minute rating. Maximum continuous has no time limit but is intended for emergency use, such as one engine inoperative.'},
 {id:'lim-008',ch:'lim',lvl:3,q:'At ground idle the ITT approaches its idle limit on a hot day. What can bring it down?',a:['Move the propeller levers to feather','Select reverse to increase airflow','Turn on autoignition to cool the burner','Reduce accessory load or raise N1'],c:3,e:'High idle ITT usually means the gas generator is turning too slowly for the load. More N1 means more cooling airflow, and less load means less fuel needed.'},
 {id:'lim-009',ch:'lim',lvl:1,q:'What is the limit on operating an engine with its FUEL PRESS warning illuminated?',a:['No limit, provided the standby pump is switched on','1 hour, then the engine-driven boost pump must be replaced','150 hours, the same limit as for avgas operation','10 hours, then the HP fuel pump must be overhauled or replaced'],c:3,e:'Low inlet pressure can cavitate and damage the high-pressure pump, so operating time with the light on is limited.'},
 {id:'lim-010',ch:'lim',lvl:2,q:'Which statement about flight on aviation gasoline is correct?',a:['Emergency only: 10 h per overhaul; crossfeed prohibited','Emergency only: 150 h per overhaul; standby pumps on above 20,000 ft','Prohibited in all circumstances for the PT6A-42','Normal fuel: no time limit at all when mixed at least 50/50 with Jet A'],c:1,e:'Avgas is allowed only as an emergency fuel with these restrictions. Record how much was loaded so the overhaul time can be charged correctly.'},
 {id:'lim-011',ch:'lim',lvl:1,q:'Up to what altitude may the B200 be flown with the yaw damper inoperative?',a:['25,000 ft','10,000 ft','35,000 ft','17,000 ft'],c:3,e:'An operating yaw damper is required above 17,000 ft.'},
 {id:'lim-012',ch:'lim',lvl:1,q:'What is the transient limit for propeller rpm?',a:['2,200 rpm for no more than 5 seconds','2,120 rpm for 30 seconds','2,300 rpm for 10 seconds','2,000 rpm, with no transient allowed at all'],c:0,e:'2,200 rpm is the absolute overspeed limit and is time-limited to 5 seconds. Steady overspeeds are covered by the overspeed-governor rules.'},
 {id:'lim-013',ch:'lim',lvl:1,q:'What are the low-idle limits for a typical late-serial B200 engine?',a:['N1 at least 61 %; ITT no more than 750°C','N1 at least 70 %; ITT no more than 800°C','N1 at least 56 %; ITT no more than 850°C','N1 at least 52 %; ITT no more than 660°C'],c:0,e:'The four-blade later B200 has a 61 % low-idle minimum, and ITT at low idle is limited to 750°C.'},
 {id:'lim-014',ch:'lim',lvl:2,q:'Which statement about starter and generator limits is correct?',a:['Starter 40/60 s, unlimited cycles; gen 88 % to FL310, 100 % above, 85 % ground','Starter 60/60/60/60 s then 15 min off; gen 100 % everywhere in flight, 85 % ground','Starter 40/60/40/60/40 s then 30 min off; gen 100 % to FL310, 88 % above, 85 % ground','Starter 30 s on, 5 min off; gen 90 % in flight and 75 % on the ground'],c:2,e:'Both limits protect the starter-generator from overheating. On the ground there are also minimum N1 values linked to generator load.'},
 {id:'lim-015',ch:'lim',lvl:1,q:'What is the minimum fuel quantity on either gauge for take-off?',a:['About 400 lb','100 lb','500 lb','265 lb'],c:3,e:'Take-off is prohibited with less than 265 lb on either side (yellow arc).'},
 {id:'lim-016',ch:'lim',lvl:2,q:'What is the limit on fuel imbalance, and where should you look for any extra restriction?',a:['2,000 lb; the MEL may set a lower value for dispatch','1,000 lb; supplements may set a lower value','500 lb; no other document applies','No limit while crossfeed is serviceable'],c:1,e:'1,000 lb is the basic limit. Supplements can be more restrictive, for example with the autopilot engaged.'},
 {id:'lim-017',ch:'lim',lvl:2,q:'Which limits apply to ground and reverse use of the propellers?',a:['Reverse 1,900 rpm for 1 min; never into beta/reverse when stopped or feathered','Reverse 2,200 rpm for 5 min; beta allowed in flight below 140 KIAS','Reverse 2,000 rpm with no time limit; beta allowed with engines stopped','Reverse 1,900 rpm for 5 min; propeller levers at low rpm for reversing'],c:0,e:'The topping governor holds about 1,900 rpm in reverse. Lifting the levers over the gates without oil pressure damages the reverse linkage.'},
 {id:'emer-001',ch:'emer',lvl:3,q:'Engine failure after V1 on take-off: what is the overall priority order?',a:['Raise the flaps, then the gear, then rotate at V2 and accelerate','Reject the take-off and stop on the remaining runway with full reverse','Control, rotate, climb, gear up, V2, verify feather, then VYSE and clean-up','Identify and shut down the failed engine on the runway, then rotate'],c:2,e:'Above V1 the take-off continues. Control and climb come first and the checklist afterwards, normally once clear of obstacles at about 1,000 ft AGL. Use the QRH for the exact actions.'},
 {id:'emer-002',ch:'emer',lvl:3,q:'With autofeather armed, why should you NOT retard the failed engine\'s power lever right after an engine failure on take-off?',a:['It would close that engine\'s firewall valve automatically','It would cause a hot start on the failed engine','It would trigger autofeather on the operating engine instead of the failed one','It can disarm autofeather before the propeller has fully feathered'],c:3,e:'Autofeather needs the power lever to stay forward until feathering is complete. Pulling it back too early can leave a windmilling propeller and high drag.'},
 {id:'emer-003',ch:'emer',lvl:3,q:'Right after an engine failure, how do you confirm which engine has failed before taking any irreversible action?',a:['Dead foot, dead engine, confirmed by engine gauges and crew cross-check','Pull each power lever back in turn and see which one makes a difference','Wait for the fire warning or a caution to show which side failed','Look out of the window at the propellers and trust what you see'],c:0,e:'Feathering or shutting down the wrong engine can be catastrophic. Confirm with the instruments and a crew cross-check before using the condition lever or firewall valve.'},
 {id:'emer-004',ch:'emer',lvl:3,q:'Engine failure before V1 on a dry runway: what is the immediate action logic?',a:['Continue the take-off if the remaining runway looks long enough','Reject: idle, keep straight, brakes and reverse, stop, then QRH','Rotate at once and climb at VMCA until clear of obstacles','Feather the failed engine first, then decide whether to stop'],c:1,e:'Below V1 the aircraft must be able to stop within the accelerate-stop distance. Idle both levers first, keep straight, and use reverse (asymmetrically with care) and braking.'},
 {id:'emer-005',ch:'emer',lvl:3,q:'A confirmed engine fire in flight: what is the logic behind the order of actions?',a:['Increase airspeed to blow the fire out before deciding on shutdown','Fire the bottle at once, then decide later whether to shut the engine down','Cut fuel and airflow and feather first, then fire the bottle, then land (QRH)','Turn the battery off to isolate the fire, then shut the engine down'],c:2,e:'The agent is single-shot and works best once fuel is cut off and the nacelle is not being fed. Confirm the warning first and use the QRH for the exact sequence.'},
 {id:'emer-006',ch:'emer',lvl:3,q:'On a single-engine visual approach, when should you select full flaps?',a:['Only once the landing is assured','Never, with one engine inoperative','On downwind, together with the gear','At the final approach fix'],c:0,e:'Full flaps add a lot of drag. Delaying them keeps go-around capability and control margin until you are committed. Bring the propeller lever full forward early so a go-around is ready.'},
 {id:'emer-007',ch:'emer',lvl:3,q:'During a single-engine approach a go-around becomes necessary. What are the key elements?',a:['Gear up first, then add power, then climb at VREF with full flaps','Keep full flaps and gear down until 1,000 ft, then clean up at VMCA','Restart the failed engine first, then add power on both engines','Max power, control, positive climb, gear up, flaps up, VYSE, QRH'],c:3,e:'Power and control come first, then remove drag in an order that keeps a positive climb. Below VYSE climb performance is poor, which is why the decision should be made early.'},
 {id:'emer-008',ch:'emer',lvl:3,q:'Smoke with an electrical smell appears in the cockpit in cruise. What is the general priority order?',a:['Turn off all electrical power, including the battery, in IMC at once','Open the cabin door slightly in flight to clear the smoke quickly','Masks on and communicate, then isolate the source per QRH and land soon','Search for the source first, then put on masks if the smoke thickens'],c:2,e:'Protect the crew first, because smoke can disable you quickly. Then isolate the source using the QRH and plan an early landing.'},
 {id:'emer-009',ch:'emer',lvl:3,q:'During an emergency descent from high altitude, what typical configuration and speed logic is used?',a:['Idle, props back to 1,600 rpm, gear up, flaps up, descend at VMO','Max continuous power, gear up, flaps up, and descend at VMO all the way','Feather both propellers for drag, gear down, and descend at VLE','Idle, props forward, approach flaps below 200, gear below 181, up to VLE'],c:3,e:'Idle power and high rpm give strong propeller drag, and the gear and flaps add more. That allows a steep descent at a controlled speed; going from 35,000 ft to 12,500 ft takes roughly six minutes.'},
 {id:'emer-010',ch:'emer',lvl:3,q:'You need an emergency descent, but your airspeed is above VLE. What do you do first?',a:['Slow to the gear and flap limits first, then configure','Descend clean at VMO without configuring at all','Lower the gear immediately, whatever the airspeed happens to be','Feather one propeller to slow down more quickly'],c:0,e:'Exceeding VLE or VFE can damage the gear or flaps. Slow to the limits first, then configure.'},
 {id:'emer-011',ch:'emer',lvl:3,q:'The gear does not extend normally on approach. What is the general approach?',a:['Cycle the gear handle repeatedly until the gear finally extends','Land gear-up straight away to save fuel and time on the approach','Fly, break off if needed, check basics, then QRH alternate extension','Pull every gear circuit breaker and reset them one by one'],c:2,e:'Make time and keep the aircraft under control. Follow the QRH steps in order. On hydraulic-gear aircraft this means the LANDING GEAR RELAY breaker, handle DN and the hand pump.'},
 {id:'emer-012',ch:'emer',lvl:3,q:'An amber CHIP DETECT caution comes on for one engine in cruise on a later B200. How should you think about it?',a:['Fuel system advisory only: no action needed until after landing','Possible gearbox wear: monitor closely, QRH, consider precautionary shutdown','Oil filter bypassing: increase power to raise the oil flow and pressure on that engine','Fire risk: discharge that engine\'s fire bottle immediately'],c:1,e:'A chip light is a warning sign, not proof of failure. Watch oil pressure, temperature and vibration, and follow the QRH and your operator\'s guidance.'},
 {id:'emer-013',ch:'emer',lvl:3,q:'Oil pressure on one engine drops below 60 psi in cruise. What does the limitation require?',a:['Shut down, or land at the closest suitable airport at minimum power (QRH)','Reduce torque to 1,100 ft-lb and continue the planned flight to destination','Increase propeller rpm to raise oil pressure into the green','Continue to destination at normal power and monitor the gauge'],c:0,e:'Below 60 psi is unsafe, and the engine may fail. The QRH decides whether to keep it running at minimum power or to secure it.'},
 {id:'emer-014',ch:'emer',lvl:3,q:'The propeller rpm rises well above 2,120 rpm and does not respond to the propeller lever. What does this suggest, and what is the priority?',a:['Add power to load the propeller, which pulls the rpm back down again','Fuel topping governor self-test in progress: it resets in a few seconds','Normal overspeed governor test in progress: no action is needed at all','Both governors may have failed: cut power and speed, consider shutdown (QRH)'],c:3,e:'Steady overspeeds above the overspeed-governor setting are unapproved. Reducing power and airspeed unloads the propeller, and the QRH guides the decision to secure the engine.'},
 {id:'emer-015',ch:'emer',lvl:3,q:'With AUTOIGNITION armed, an engine briefly loses power in heavy rain and then recovers. What most likely happened?',a:['The fire extinguisher fired and cleared the problem','The FCU switched to a manual mode and back','The propeller briefly feathered and then unfeathered by itself','Torque fell below 400 ft-lb, the igniters fired, and it relit'],c:3,e:'Autoignition protects against flameouts from short fuel or air interruptions. Afterwards, check the engine indications and follow the QRH if anything looks abnormal.'},
 {id:'emer-016',ch:'emer',lvl:3,q:'Approaching to land, you meet a microburst with a rapid increase in sink. Which recovery technique applies?',a:['Reduce power to avoid an overspeed in the downdraught','Lower the nose to gain speed, then climb once the speed has built up again','Max power, about 10° nose-up, no configuration change until climbing','Retract the flaps immediately to reduce drag, then add power'],c:2,e:'Climbing out of the shear is the aim, and the gear stays down until the climb is confirmed. Avoidance is the best defence.'},
 {id:'emer-017',ch:'emer',lvl:3,q:'Both generators fail at night in IMC and will not reset. What should you do with the electrical system?',a:['Shed to essentials, keep nav and approach items, land soon (QRH)','Leave everything on; the battery will last for several hours','Switch inverters repeatedly to restore generator power','Turn the battery off now to save it for the landing'],c:0,e:'The battery is now the only source and its endurance is limited. Planned load shedding keeps the important items (communication, navigation, lights, gear) available for the landing.'},
 {id:'emer-018',ch:'emer',lvl:3,q:'After closing a bleed-air valve because of a BL AIR FAIL warning, which follow-on effects should you anticipate?',a:['That engine will flame out within a few minutes from lack of air','Light stays on, rudder boost disarmed, that side\'s brake deice off (QRH)','Light goes out after a minute and every system returns to normal','The gear can now only be extended with the hand pump (QRH)'],c:1,e:'Closing a bleed valve affects several systems. Anticipating those effects, especially the loss of rudder boost if an engine then fails, is good situational awareness.'},
 {id:'emer-019',ch:'emer',lvl:3,q:'The windshield ices over at 240 KIAS in severe icing, with windshield heat on. What should you do first?',a:['Increase speed to melt the ice','Cycle the windshield heat off and on','Slow to 226 KIAS or less','Run the wipers on FAST'],c:2,e:'Above about 226 KIAS the heaters may not keep up in severe icing. Slowing lets them work, while keeping at least 140 KIAS to avoid ice on the underside of the wing.'},
 {id:'emer-020',ch:'emer',lvl:3,q:'Why does the QRH matter even for well-practised memory items?',a:['It confirms the memory items and adds follow-up steps and limits','It replaces the AFM limitations for all abnormal and emergency situations','It only matters if the memory items have failed to work','It is only for the copilot to read during the approach'],c:0,e:'Train the logic, but in the aircraft use your operator\'s QRH and AFM as the authority. This question bank teaches concepts, not an operator\'s exact wording.'},
 {id:'emer-021',ch:'emer',lvl:3,q:'During cruise the cabin altitude rises slowly despite normal bleed settings. The ALT WARN is not on yet. What is a reasonable plan?',a:['Climb higher to increase the cabin differential','Check bleed, CABIN PRESS and controller; if it keeps rising, descend early','Wait until the ALT WARN light comes on before taking any action or descending','Select CABIN PRESS to DUMP to reset the controller'],c:1,e:'A slow loss gives you time to troubleshoot, but plan ahead. Descending early keeps the cabin below the 12,500 ft warning level and protects the passengers.'},
 {id:'emer-022',ch:'emer',lvl:3,q:'The red INVERTER warning appears and switching inverters does not restore AC. What should you think about on a later B200?',a:['Expect both propellers to feather, because the propeller governors need AC power','Identify the AC users and cross-check; later-serial engine gauges are DC','Expect to lose all engine instruments, because they run on AC','Expect both generators to trip off, because they need AC'],c:1,e:'On later serials the engine instruments are DC-powered, while earlier aircraft used AC for some gauges. Knowing what you lose helps you prioritise.'},
 {id:'emer-023',ch:'emer',lvl:3,q:'An engine fails at cruise altitude and the propeller has been feathered. What should you consider before attempting a restart?',a:['The fuel state only: restart if more than 1,000 lb remains','The cause: no restart after fire, mechanical damage or oil loss (QRH)','Nothing: always restart at once to keep the engine warm','The time only: wait 30 minutes so the engine can cool'],c:1,e:'A restart can help only if the cause is benign, such as a fuel interruption. The QRH sets the conditions, and remember that the propeller unfeathers only once the engine is turning.'},
 {id:'emer-024',ch:'emer',lvl:3,q:'Shortly after take-off, at 600 ft AGL, the left engine fails and autofeather feathers it. Your speed is 5 kt below V2 and decreasing. What is the priority?',a:['Lower the nose to hold V2, keep control, checklists later (QRH)','Raise the flaps immediately to reduce drag, then hold altitude','Pull back to hold altitude whatever happens to the speed','Restart the left engine before doing anything else'],c:0,e:'Speed below V2 erodes both climb performance and control margin. Flying the target speed comes before any checklist. Clean-up and the QRH follow once you are clear of obstacles.'},
 {id:'emer-025',ch:'emer',lvl:3,q:'On a single-engine ILS you are fully configured with flaps down at 200 ft above minimums when ATC reports the runway blocked. What is the main consideration for the go-around?',a:['Reduce power to stay below VMCA, then climb slowly at full flaps','Very limited climb: max power, control, clean up per QRH while climbing','Keep full flaps and gear down until 1,000 ft, then clean up at VMCA','Bank 30° towards the failed engine to turn away from the runway'],c:1,e:'This is why full flaps are normally delayed until the landing is assured on one engine. If you must go around, power, control and drag reduction come first, guided by the QRH.'}
];
